#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ccrc-doctor-checks — the check TABLE `ccrc doctor` iterates, and each check's
# implementation. Stage 2b of the 2026-08-15-stage2b-ccrc-cli-and-doctor plan:
# the PREREQUISITE checks (Task 4), roster/wrapper coherence (Task 5, the
# `wrappers` entry) and the cross-box matched set (Task 7, `fleet`, the last
# entry in the table and the last function in this file).
#
# SOURCED, never executed — `ccrc`'s `cmd_doctor` does `. "$CCRC_HERE/ccrc-doctor-checks"`.
# It in turn sources `ccrc-wrapper-shape` (see below), so **DOCTOR's set is
# THREE files in one directory** — `ccrc`, this file, `ccrc-wrapper-shape` —
# not two. THE CLI'S SET IS FOUR: `ccrc-adopt` joins them, because `cmd_adopt`
# execs it (Task 8). Both counts are correct and they are counting different
# things; `ccrc`'s own header counts the CLI's four, and whatever installs the
# CLI must ship all four. The two paragraphs used to share one wording, which
# read as a contradiction.
# Split out of `ccrc` itself (plan, "Files") so a check is testable without the
# CLI's whole argument surface, and so the table stays visible as data rather
# than buried in a dispatch.
#
# ── THE CONTRACT EVERY CHECK KEEPS ────────────────────────────────────────
# `_check_<name>` prints a verdict line to STDOUT
#
#     PASS|WARN|FAIL <name>: <detail>
#
# and, when the verdict is not PASS, exactly one further line
#
#     "  remedy: <what to do about it>"
#
# and returns 0 (pass), 2 (warn) or 1 (fail). The remedy is not optional and
# not advisory: `_dr_fail`/`_dr_warn` print BOTH lines themselves, so a check
# physically cannot emit a non-pass verdict without one. A check that only
# complains sends an operator to a search engine; the remedy is the half that
# makes doctor an instrument instead of an alarm.
#
# ── THE FOURTH OUTCOME: A CHECK WITH NOTHING TO MEASURE ───────────────────
# A check whose SUBJECT does not exist on this box prints, instead,
#
#     SKIP <name>: <why there was nothing to measure>
#
# with no remedy, and returns 3 (`_dr_skip`). `fleet` is the case that forced
# it: it compares TWO boxes, and a box with no server to ask has no second box.
# PASS would claim an agreement nobody measured — the overloaded-null defect
# this codebase bans by name — and WARN would report a problem where there is
# none. `ccrc`'s `cmd_doctor` counts a skip against CHECKS and never against
# verdicts, and prints the count, so "N checks, M verdicts" says how much of
# the box was really measured.
#
# NO REMEDY UNDER A SKIP, deliberately: a `remedy:` line reads as "there is
# something here to fix", and on the fleet host — which has no `ccrc.env` and
# no reason to know where the server is — there is not. Where an operator COULD
# make the check answer, that goes in the detail, as a fact about what is
# missing rather than as an instruction.
#
# ── ONE CHECK MAY ANSWER IN TWO (OR MORE) CLASSES ─────────────────────────
# Almost every check prints exactly ONE verdict line. A check that has found
# findings of two different classes may print a FAIL line and then a WARN line
# — each with its own remedy — and returns the WORSE class (1). It is not a
# licence to be chatty; it is the rule that stops the overloaded seam this
# codebase bans by name. `_check_wrappers` is the case that forced it (Task 5
# review, Important 1): "your roster and this wrapper disagree, fix one of
# them" and "here is a launcher nobody wrote down, which may well be
# deliberate" are two findings an operator acts on DIFFERENTLY, and joining
# them into one FAIL sentence both laundered the second into a defect and
# discarded the remedy that belonged to it.
#
# TWO FAIL LINES from one check is the same rule, not an exception to it:
# `_check_wrappers` again (D-82, Task 1 of stage2d) — "nothing exists on disk
# for `ccrc wrappers` to refuse" and "the roster and the wrapper on disk
# disagree" are BOTH hard findings (both FAIL, both return 1), but they are
# still two DIFFERENT operator actions (`ccrc wrappers` vs hand-reconciling
# the roster), so they get two FAIL lines, each with its own remedy, rather
# than one bucket whose remedy is right for only one of the two sentences it
# joined. The worst-class rule does not change: two FAILs still return 1, the
# same as one would.
#
# THREE FAIL LINES from one check is the same rule again, still not an
# exception: `_check_wrappers` a third time (A2-NEW) — an absent upstream
# binary ("go install Claude Code") joins the other two FAIL classes above as
# a THIRD hard finding with a THIRD distinct operator action. `cmd_doctor`'s
# line-counting below is not a fixed arity: it scans a check's captured output
# line by line, counts every `FAIL <name>: `/`WARN <name>: ` it sees (however
# many there are), and cross-checks only whether ANY FAIL line was printed
# against the return code (worst = FAIL if the FAIL count is nonzero) — so a
# check answering in four classes at once (this one, on a box that is BOTH
# missing its upstream binary and disagreeing about a generated one) needs no
# change there. Adding a bucket costs the bucket's own `if`, not a change to
# what counts it.
#
# `ccrc`'s `cmd_doctor` counts VERDICT LINES, not return codes, so a check that
# answers in several classes is counted in all of them — and it cross-checks
# the loudest statement against the return code: a return code that is not the
# worst class the check printed is reported as a bug in ccrc.
#
# The verdict lines are the RESULT of the verb, so they go to stdout — NOT to
# stderr through `_ccrc_say`, which `ccrc`'s own header (Task 1) had guessed
# they would. The pairing "a FAIL line, then ITS remedy on the next line" is
# the machine-readable part of this contract (`server/test/ccrc-doctor.test.ts`
# asserts it per FAIL line), and splitting the pair across two streams would
# break it for anyone who redirects one of them — which is every caller that
# pipes doctor into a report. `_ccrc_say` keeps its job: stderr notes that are
# not a verdict.
#
# The printed <name> IS the function suffix (`_check_gh_auth` prints `gh_auth`),
# so a reader who sees a line can grep straight to the code that wrote it.
# That is why the names are underscored rather than hyphenated.
#
# ── WHY THE DETAIL IS ALWAYS A MEASUREMENT ────────────────────────────────
# Every PASS names what was actually found (the resolved path, the version, the
# address), never the bare word "ok". A check that prints "PASS tmux: ok" is
# indistinguishable from a check that was never wired up, and this project has
# already been bitten by exactly that shape (`single-definition.test.ts`'s "a
# scan over an empty list passes everything").
#
# ── set -e ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file is sourced into `ccrc`, which runs `set -uo pipefail` and NOT `-e`,
# deliberately — see that file's header. Checks SIGNAL by returning non-zero;
# under `-e` the first failing check would abort the run, which is precisely
# the run an operator needs to see whole. Nothing here may turn `-e` back on.

# Where the shipped tree is, relative to THIS file rather than to $PWD —
# `ccrc-adopt:139-140`'s idiom, in the same PATH-free spelling `ccrc` uses (see
# that file for why `dirname` is not called here: doctor has to work on a box
# whose PATH is the broken thing). `${BASH_SOURCE[0]}` and not `$0`, because
# this file is only ever reached by `source`, where `$0` is the CALLER's
# argv[0]. `:=` so the value `ccrc` already computed wins when there is one,
# and so this file still resolves itself when a test sources it on its own.
if [ -z "${CCRC_HERE:-}" ]; then
  CCRC_HERE="${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
  [[ "$CCRC_HERE" == */* ]] || CCRC_HERE="./$CCRC_HERE"
  CCRC_HERE="$(cd "${CCRC_HERE%/*}" && pwd)"
fi

# ── the wrapper contract, sourced rather than restated ────────────────────
# `ccrc-wrapper-shape` is the THIRD file that ships beside `ccrc`, and it is
# load-bearing rather than incidental: it holds the shape of a generated
# account wrapper, which `ccrc-adopt` writes rosters FROM and `_check_wrappers`
# checks rosters AGAINST. A second copy of that shape is how the two would
# drift into agreeing with each other about a shape neither tool produces —
# which would make the check worse than useless.
#
# Sourced BEST-EFFORT, not with `||` refusal, on purpose: a missing library
# must not take the other ten checks down with it. `_check_wrappers` notices
# its absence itself and says so in a verdict line with a remedy — see the top
# of that function, and `ccrc-doctor.test.ts`'s "did not ship" case, which is
# the mechanism that keeps the install honest.
CCRC_WRAPPER_LIB="$CCRC_HERE/ccrc-wrapper-shape"
if [ -r "$CCRC_WRAPPER_LIB" ]; then
  # shellcheck source=ccrc-wrapper-shape
  . "$CCRC_WRAPPER_LIB"
fi

# How long `gh auth status` may take. Overridable for the same reason
# `deploy/verify-service.sh:56-62` makes its settle/window knobs overridable: so
# a test need not wait out a production timeout. The DEFAULT is what an operator
# gets. (The OTHER checks that touch the network, `build` and `fleet`, are
# bounded by `CCRC_HEALTH_TIMEOUT`, declared in `ccrc` beside the reader that
# uses it — `ccrc status` makes the same call and must be bounded by the same
# knob.)
: "${CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT:=15}"

# ── the check table ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATA. `cmd_doctor` iterates this array and calls `_check_<name>` for each
# entry, so adding a check is one entry here, one function below, and one test
# — and `ccrc-doctor.test.ts`'s first case fails the build if the table and the
# functions ever disagree in either direction.
CCRC_DOCTOR_CHECKS=(
  node
  tmux
  tmux_skew
  git
  gh
  jq
  python3
  flock
  gh_auth
  git_email
  linger
  path
  services
  config
  auth
  rc
  exposure
  caddy
  caddyfile
  cert
  name
  disk
  wrappers
  build
  fleet
)

# ── verdict helpers ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Newlines are squashed out of both halves: the contract is "the line
# immediately after a non-PASS verdict is its remedy", and a detail carrying an
# embedded newline (a subprocess's multi-line stderr, say) would silently break
# that pairing for every reader.
_dr_line() { printf '%s %s: %s\n' "$1" "$2" "${3//$'\n'/ }"; }
_dr_pass() { _dr_line PASS "$1" "$2"; return 0; }
_dr_warn() { _dr_line WARN "$1" "$2"; printf '  remedy: %s\n' "${3//$'\n'/ }"; return 2; }
_dr_fail() { _dr_line FAIL "$1" "$2"; printf '  remedy: %s\n' "${3//$'\n'/ }"; return 1; }
# No remedy, by contract — see "THE FOURTH OUTCOME" in this file's header.
_dr_skip() { _dr_line SKIP "$1" "$2"; return 3; }

# Findings joined onto one verdict line with "; ". Built here rather than with
# `printf '%s; '` so the last one carries no trailing separator. SHARED between
# `_check_wrappers` and `_check_fleet` (it was `_dr_wr_join`, wrappers-owned,
# until the second caller arrived): a private second copy of four lines of
# string-joining is exactly the drift this file's own header objects to in the
# large, and there is nothing account-specific about it.
_dr_join() {
  local out="" x
  for x in "$@"; do [ -z "$out" ] || out+="; "; out+="$x"; done
  printf '%s' "$out"
}

# Leading/trailing whitespace off a captured value, in pure parameter
# expansion (no `sed`, no subshell — see `_dr_pkg_candidates` for why this file
# avoids PATH lookups it does not need). Two callers, and BOTH were defects the
# review found: a floor of `" >=22.13.0 "` was rejected by doctor while
# node-floor.test.ts:38-39 accepts it (it does `.exec(range.trim())` and this
# claimed to copy that rule EXACTLY), and a `user.email` of `"   "` was
# non-empty enough to PASS — printing `PASS git_email:    `, a line this
# suite's own shape test declares illegal, for a box whose commits carry an
# empty author address.
_dr_trim() {
  local s="$1"
  s="${s#"${s%%[![:space:]]*}"}"
  s="${s%"${s##*[![:space:]]}"}"
  printf '%s' "$s"
}

# First non-empty line of a captured output, for details that quote a tool's
# own diagnosis without pasting a screenful of it.
_dr_first_line() {
  local l
  while IFS= read -r l; do
    [ -n "$l" ] || continue
    printf '%s' "$l"
    return 0
  done <<< "$1"
  printf '%s' ""
}

# The one shape shared by the six "is this binary installed" checks.
_dr_need_bin() {
  local name="$1" why="$2" remedy="$3" p
  p="$(command -v "$name" 2>/dev/null)"
  if [ -z "$p" ]; then
    _dr_fail "$name" "not on PATH — $why" "$remedy"
    return 1
  fi
  _dr_pass "$name" "$p"
}

# ── node: the floor is READ, never re-typed ───────────────────────────────
# WHICH package.json IS CANONICAL DEPENDS ON THE BOX, and that is why this
# looks in two places rather than one:
#   - a fleet host has `~/ccrc/{agent,shared,deploy,ccd}` (deploy.sh:343), so
#     the shipped declaration there is `~/ccrc/agent/package.json`;
#   - a server host has `~/ccrc/{server,shared,deploy,ccd}` (deploy.sh:613), so
#     it is `~/ccrc/server/package.json`. `ccd` is in BOTH lanes as of Task 8 —
#     the server box gets the directory so `ccrc` and its three siblings have a
#     home there, and it installs nothing else out of it (no ccd binary, no
#     session hooks; those are the agent lane's). This paragraph said
#     `{server,shared,deploy}` until the whole-branch review caught it: the
#     LOGIC never depended on the difference (both candidates are tried, and
#     the right one exists on each box), but a comment that is wrong about
#     what ships is exactly how the next reader mis-predicts a deploy.
#   - `pwa/package.json` never ships at all and is deliberately not consulted.
# Either answer is the SAME answer: `server/test/node-floor.test.ts:45-50`
# asserts all three declarations are byte-identical and fails the build the
# moment they are not. That test is the reason this may take the first one it
# finds instead of cross-checking them.
#
# Resolved relative to THIS SCRIPT, not to $PWD, so doctor works on a bare
# installed box with no checkout anywhere near it — which is the entire point
# of doctor (plan, "Open questions": "`ccrc doctor` must not depend on a
# checkout").
_dr_pkg_candidates() {
  local root
  root="$(cd "$CCRC_HERE/.." 2>/dev/null && pwd)" || root="$CCRC_HERE/.."
  printf '%s\n' "$root/server/package.json" "$root/agent/package.json"
}

_check_node() {
  local p
  p="$(command -v node 2>/dev/null)"
  if [ -z "$p" ]; then
    _dr_fail node "not on PATH — the server, the agent and every deploy step run on it" \
      "install Node (nodesource or nvm), then re-run: ccrc doctor"
    return 1
  fi

  local pkg="" cand
  while IFS= read -r cand; do
    [ -f "$cand" ] && { pkg="$cand"; break; }
  done < <(_dr_pkg_candidates)
  if [ -z "$pkg" ]; then
    # Joined in bash rather than through `tr`: a check that needs a coreutils
    # binary to print its own failure message is a check that goes silent on
    # exactly the broken box it exists for.
    local looked
    looked="$(_dr_pkg_candidates)"
    _dr_fail node "no shipped package.json to read engines.node from — looked at ${looked//$'\n'/ and }" \
      "run ccrc from inside the shipped tree (~/ccrc/ccd/ccrc) or from a checkout; a lone copy of this script cannot know the floor"
    return 1
  fi

  # Parsed by node itself, not by jq: the node check must not fail for a reason
  # that belongs to another check (a box with no jq gets exactly one FAIL, from
  # the jq check), and node is by definition available here — its absence is
  # the branch above. The path travels in the environment rather than argv so
  # there is no argv[1]-vs-argv[2] ambiguity to get wrong.
  # READING the file and PARSING it are separated on purpose (Task 4 review,
  # Minor 6): `chmod 000` and "somebody wrote garbage here" are two conditions
  # an operator acts on completely differently — one is a mode/ownership fix,
  # the other a redeploy — and collapsing them into one signal is the
  # overloaded-seam mistake this codebase bans by name. It is the same defect
  # Task 1 fixed when a missing `jq` was being reported as a corrupt build
  # stamp. The read failure carries node's own errno out on stdout so the
  # verdict can name it.
  #   exit 5 = cannot be read (prints "READERR:<code>")
  #   exit 3 = does not parse; exit 4 = declares no engines.node.
  local range rc
  range="$(CCRC_DOCTOR_PKG="$pkg" node -e '
    const fs = require("fs");
    let text;
    try { text = fs.readFileSync(process.env.CCRC_DOCTOR_PKG, "utf8"); }
    catch (e) { process.stdout.write("READERR:" + (e && e.code ? e.code : "unknown")); process.exit(5); }
    let j;
    try { j = JSON.parse(text); }
    catch (e) { process.exit(3); }
    const r = j && j.engines && j.engines.node;
    if (typeof r !== "string" || r.length === 0) process.exit(4);
    process.stdout.write(r);
  ' 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -eq 5 ]; then
    local why="${range#READERR:}"
    [ -n "$why" ] && [ "$why" != "$range" ] || why="unreadable"
    _dr_fail node "$pkg exists but cannot be read ($why), so the floor cannot be read" \
      "fix its mode/ownership — check with: ls -l $pkg (a deploy leaves it world-readable)"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$rc" -eq 3 ]; then
    _dr_fail node "$pkg does not parse as JSON, so the floor cannot be read" \
      "restore or redeploy $pkg — something wrote garbage where a package manifest belongs"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || [ -z "$range" ]; then
    _dr_fail node "$pkg declares no engines.node, so this box has no floor to check against" \
      "declare it — engines.node is the single place the Node floor is written down (server/test/node-floor.test.ts)"
    return 1
  fi

  # EXACTLY node-floor.test.ts:38-42's rule, and for its stated reason: the
  # moment someone writes `^22 || >=24`, this must fail loudly rather than
  # silently accept a floor it did not understand. No version number is spelled
  # in this file — that is what reading the manifest is for.
  #
  # `_dr_trim` is part of that rule, not a nicety: node-floor matches against
  # `range.trim()`, so `" >=22.13.0 "` keeps all three declarations identical
  # and that suite green — a doctor that rejected it would be STRICTER than the
  # rule it claims to copy, and would fail a box whose manifests are fine. The
  # verdict below still quotes the value as written, so an otherwise invisible
  # stray space is still visible when the form really is wrong.
  local floor_re='^>=([0-9]+)\.([0-9]+)\.([0-9]+)$' declared
  declared="$(_dr_trim "$range")"
  if [[ ! "$declared" =~ $floor_re ]]; then
    _dr_fail node "unrecognised engines range \"$range\" in $pkg — this check understands only a single '>=x.y.z'" \
      "declare engines.node as one '>=x.y.z' range (node-floor.test.ts:38-42 pins the same rule), or teach ccrc-doctor-checks the new form deliberately"
    return 1
  fi
  local maj="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}" min="${BASH_REMATCH[2]}" pat="${BASH_REMATCH[3]}"

  local running ver_re='^v?([0-9]+)\.([0-9]+)\.([0-9]+)'
  running="$(node --version 2>/dev/null)"
  if [[ ! "$running" =~ $ver_re ]]; then
    _dr_fail node "'node --version' printed \"$running\", which is not a version" \
      "check what $p actually is — a wrapper script on PATH shadowing the real interpreter is the usual cause"
    return 1
  fi
  local rmaj="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}" rmin="${BASH_REMATCH[2]}" rpat="${BASH_REMATCH[3]}"

  if (( rmaj > maj || (rmaj == maj && (rmin > min || (rmin == min && rpat >= pat))) )); then
    _dr_pass node "$running satisfies $declared (declared in $pkg), at $p"
  else
    _dr_fail node "$running is below the floor $declared declared in $pkg" \
      "upgrade Node to at least ${maj}.${min}.${pat} — below it the server does not degrade, it fails to boot (node:sqlite is imported unconditionally)"
    return 1
  fi
}

# ── the binaries a fleet box cannot work without ──────────────────────────
_check_tmux() {
  _dr_need_bin tmux "every ccd session lives in a tmux window, and that is how one is attached, read and driven" \
    "install it: sudo apt install tmux"
}

# ── tmux_skew: the loaded gun — a new client under the running server ─────
# `tmux -V` names the CLIENT: the binary on disk, the one every next attach
# and every `ccd` call runs. `display-message -p '#{version}'` asks the
# RUNNING SERVER for its own. Disagreement means an upgrade landed under a
# live server, and the moment a new client speaks to it the server refuses
# the whole conversation ("protocol version mismatch") — on a fleet host that
# severs the console from every session at once, while the sessions
# themselves keep running unattached (the substrate-unreachable spec §5 calls
# this the loaded gun, seen before it fires). A version FLOOR is deliberately
# NOT checked: the dangerous direction is client/server skew, which is
# instant and total, not slow drift below some minimum.
#
# WARN and not FAIL, because the gun is loaded and not fired: nothing is
# broken while no new client starts, and the remedy is a scheduled action
# ("at the next quiet moment"), not an emergency.
#
# The server probe is deadline-bounded by CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT — the same
# knob that bounds this file's other could-hang probe (`gh_auth`), reused
# rather than cloned because both mean one thing: how long doctor may sit on
# a call that should answer instantly. A wedged server holding the socket
# without answering is precisely a box this check will be run on.
_check_tmux_skew() {
  # Absence is the `tmux` check's FAIL, one entry up — a second verdict here
  # would count the same finding twice, and with no client there is no
  # client/server pair to compare.
  if ! command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_skip tmux_skew "tmux is not on PATH (the tmux check FAILs on that), so there is no client/server pair to compare"
    return 3
  fi

  local vline client
  vline="$(tmux -V 2>/dev/null)"
  client="$(_dr_trim "${vline#tmux }")"
  if [ -z "$client" ]; then
    _dr_skip tmux_skew "'tmux -V' printed \"$vline\", which names no client version, so there is nothing to compare a server against"
    return 3
  fi

  local server rc
  server="$(_plat_timeout "$CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT" tmux display-message -p '#{version}' 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -eq 124 ]; then
    # NOT the "no server" sentence below: a server that holds the socket
    # without answering and a socket nobody holds are two different facts
    # about the box, and collapsing them is the overloaded seam this file
    # bans by name. Still a skip — nothing was measured — but the detail
    # names which nothing.
    _dr_skip tmux_skew "a tmux server holds the socket but did not answer within ${CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT}s, so its version could not be read"
    return 3
  fi
  server="$(_dr_trim "$server")"
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || [ -z "$server" ]; then
    _dr_skip tmux_skew "no tmux server is running, so there is nothing to skew against — the next client to start one will be this client"
    return 3
  fi

  if [ "$server" = "$client" ]; then
    _dr_pass tmux_skew "client $client, running server $server — versions agree"
    return 0
  fi
  _dr_warn tmux_skew "the client on disk is $client but the running server is $server" \
    "a tmux upgrade landed under the running server — restart the tmux server at the next quiet moment or the next new client will be refused"
}

_check_git() {
  _dr_need_bin git "ccd builds every workspace as a git worktree" \
    "install it: sudo apt install git"
}
_check_gh() {
  _dr_need_bin gh "PR state, review and merge all go through the GitHub CLI (ccd:700-720)" \
    "install it: see https://cli.github.com (Debian/Ubuntu: sudo apt install gh)"
}
_check_jq() {
  _dr_need_bin jq "ccrc and ccrc-adopt read this box's small JSON files with it (ccrc version, accounts.json)" \
    "install it: sudo apt install jq"
}
_check_python3() {
  _dr_need_bin python3 "ccd's session hook and its registry locking are python3 (ccd:1043-1110)" \
    "install it: sudo apt install python3"
}
_check_flock() {
  _dr_need_bin flock "ccd serialises registry writes with it, and refuses to run ws-restore without it (ccd:2501-2502)" \
    "install it: sudo apt install util-linux"
}

# ── gh's authentication ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM ccd, do not "harmonise" the two: `ccd` never
# probes `gh auth status` — it classifies gh's own stderr instead (ccd:707-715,
# "`gh auth status` is NOT probed first: it is another network call on the
# unhappy path"), because ccd runs that path in a poll loop over every open PR.
# Doctor is the COLD path: it runs when a human asks "is this box fit to
# work?", once, and the whole point of it is to answer questions the hot path
# cannot afford to ask. Moving this call into ccd would put a network round
# trip back into that loop.
#
# `--show-token` is NEVER passed, and only the FIRST line of gh's output is
# ever quoted back: gh redacts the token itself (`Token: gho_****`), and this
# file must not be the thing that un-redacts it.
_check_gh_auth() {
  if ! command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail gh_auth "gh is not on PATH, so this box's GitHub credentials cannot be checked" \
      "install gh first — see the 'gh' check above; then: gh auth login --scopes repo"
    return 1
  fi

  # `--hostname github.com`: this check's job is one host's credential, and
  # `gh auth status` with no host filter prints EVERY host this box is logged
  # into concatenated together. Task 1 of stage2d (D-82's neighbour) measured
  # the consequence — a second host's `Token scopes: 'repo'` satisfied the
  # `case` match below even when github.com's own section had no 'repo' at
  # all, which is a false PASS on exactly the credential the remedy already
  # names (`gh auth refresh -h github.com -s repo` is scoped to github.com;
  # the check that approved it should be too).
  local out rc
    out="$(_plat_timeout "$CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT" gh auth status --hostname github.com 2>&1)"; rc=$?

  if [ "$rc" -eq 124 ]; then
    _dr_fail gh_auth "gh auth status did not answer within ${CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT}s" \
      "check this box's network/proxy — an authenticated gh answers in well under a second"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    _dr_fail gh_auth "gh auth status exited $rc: $(_dr_first_line "$out")" \
      "run: gh auth login --scopes repo (and re-run ccrc doctor)"
    return 1
  fi

  case "$out" in
    *"Token scopes:"*)
      # Matched WITH its quotes, exactly as gh prints a scope list, so
      # 'repo:status' — a read-only scope — cannot be mistaken for 'repo'.
      case "$out" in
        *"'repo'"*)
          _dr_pass gh_auth "authenticated, and the token carries the 'repo' scope" ;;
        *)
          _dr_fail gh_auth "authenticated, but the token has no 'repo' scope — this box could not open or merge a PR" \
            "run: gh auth refresh -h github.com -s repo"
          return 1 ;;
      esac
      ;;
    *)
      # A third state, and it must not collapse into either neighbour: PASS
      # would claim a fact nobody measured, FAIL would block a box that may be
      # entirely fine (a GH_TOKEN from the environment reports no scope list).
      _dr_warn gh_auth "authenticated, but gh printed no 'Token scopes:' line, so the 'repo' scope could not be verified" \
        "check by hand: gh auth status — a token from GH_TOKEN or a fine-grained token must still grant contents+pull-requests write"
      return 2 ;;
  esac
}

# ── git's identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `--global`, then `--system` as a fallback: those two files ARE the box's
# identity, and nothing else counts. Deliberately NOT `git -C "$HOME" config
# user.email`, which is what this shipped first and which the review caught
# (Task 4 review, Important 1): that form reads system + global PLUS
# $HOME's own repo-local config, so the widespread dotfiles-in-$HOME pattern
# (`git init ~`) made the check PASS on a value that applies to exactly one
# repository — a false PASS on precisely the box this check exists to catch,
# and a verdict that contradicted its own FAIL text ("nothing in ~/.gitconfig
# or /etc/gitconfig"). Reproduced, then pinned by the dotfiles test in
# ccrc-doctor.test.ts.
#
# MEASURED on this project's own fleet box, and it is why the distinction
# matters rather than being pedantry: `~/.gitconfig` exists there and declares
# no user.email at all — every repository sets its own in `.git/config`. That
# box commits happily today and would refuse the FIRST commit in the next
# project ccd clones. FAIL, not WARN, for exactly that reason: the gap is
# invisible until it stops a worker mid-wave.
_check_git_email() {
  if ! command -v git >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail git_email "git is not on PATH, so this box's commit identity cannot be read" \
      "install git first — see the 'git' check above"
    return 1
  fi
  local email scope=--global
  email="$(_dr_trim "$(git config --global user.email 2>/dev/null)")"
  if [ -z "$email" ]; then
    scope=--system
    email="$(_dr_trim "$(git config --system user.email 2>/dev/null)")"
  fi
  if [ -z "$email" ]; then
    _dr_fail git_email "not set for this box (nothing in ~/.gitconfig or /etc/gitconfig) — a commit in any repository that does not set its own would be refused" \
      "run: git config --global user.email \"you@example.com\" (and user.name alongside it)"
    return 1
  fi
  _dr_pass git_email "$email (git config $scope user.email)"
}

# ── systemd linger ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Net-new: nothing in this repository has ever checked linger — it is prose in
# the planning documents and nothing more. It belongs in doctor because every
# ccd session is a `systemctl --user` unit (`ccd/claude-session@.service`), and
# without linger /run/user/$UID is torn down when the last login session ends,
# taking the whole fleet with it. `ccd:93-95` defaults XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and
# DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS so `systemctl --user` works from a non-interactive
# SSH context; this check runs in that same world and asks the question those
# defaults assume the answer to.
#
# Queried by $UID (a bash builtin variable — no `id` subprocess, and no NSS
# name lookup to fail) and remedied by UID too, so the remedy is a command an
# operator can paste verbatim.
_check_linger() {
  # macOS HAS NO LINGER, AND NO WAY TO GET ONE. `loginctl enable-linger` makes
  # a user's units run with nobody logged in; a LaunchAgent runs inside the
  # GUI login session and stops at logout. The only thing on this platform
  # that survives a logout is a LaunchDAEMON — root-owned, in
  # /Library/LaunchDaemons, running as a different user with a different
  # keychain — which is a posture nobody asked for and `ccrc install`
  # deliberately does not choose.
  #
  # SO THIS IS A WARN, NOT A FAIL, and the distinction is the whole point of
  # this arm: a FAIL says "fix this", and there is nothing to fix. The
  # operator's box is correctly installed and has one guarantee less than a
  # Linux fleet host does. The remedy names the two settings that come closest
  # to the missing guarantee rather than a command that does not exist.
  if [ "${CCD_OS:-linux}" = darwin ]; then
    _dr_warn linger "not a macOS concept — a LaunchAgent runs in your login session, so this box's server and its sessions stop at logout and start again at login. A Linux fleet host keeps them running with nobody logged in; this box cannot" \
      "for an always-on box: stay logged in, and turn off sleep (System Settings > Lock Screen, and 'Prevent automatic sleeping' in Energy/Battery)"
    return 2
  fi
  if ! command -v loginctl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail linger "loginctl is not on PATH — this box has no logind, but every ccd session is a systemd --user unit" \
      "install/enable systemd-logind, or do not run a fleet on this box"
    return 1
  fi
  local out rc
  out="$(loginctl show-user "$UID" --property=Linger 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    # The measured shape of a box that never had it: logind knows nothing about
    # a user with neither a live session nor linger, so "not enabled" arrives
    # as an error rather than as `Linger=no`. Same verdict, different detail —
    # collapsing the two would hide which one an operator is looking at.
    _dr_fail linger "logind does not know uid $UID (loginctl show-user exited $rc) — a user with neither a live session nor linger answers exactly this way" \
      "run: sudo loginctl enable-linger $UID"
    return 1
  fi
  case "${out#Linger=}" in
    yes)
      _dr_pass linger "enabled for uid $UID — /run/user/$UID survives logout, so the session units keep running" ;;
    no)
      _dr_fail linger "not enabled for uid $UID — the fleet's systemd --user units die with the last login session" \
        "run: sudo loginctl enable-linger $UID"
      return 1 ;;
    *)
      _dr_fail linger "could not read Linger from loginctl (it printed \"$out\")" \
        "check by hand: loginctl show-user $UID --property=Linger"
      return 1 ;;
  esac
}

# ── $HOME/.local/bin on PATH ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE CHECK THAT MAKES EVERY OTHER REMEDY IN THIS FILE EXECUTABLE. `ccrc`, `ccd`,
# `ccd-cap-scopes` and every generated account wrapper are installed into
# `$HOME/.local/bin` (`deploy.sh`'s `install_atomic … .local/bin/…`, and
# `ccrc-adopt`/`ccrc wrappers` write there too), so a box whose login shell never
# put that directory on PATH answers "command not found" to every instruction
# this run just printed — and to `ccrc doctor` itself the next time.
#
# WARN AND NOT FAIL, deliberately: nothing about the box is broken, and a shell
# that DOES have the directory on PATH works perfectly. What is wrong is the
# environment the operator will type the next command into.
#
# THE POSITION IS PART OF THE MEASUREMENT (see "WHY THE DETAIL IS ALWAYS A
# MEASUREMENT" above): "it is on PATH" and "it is on PATH ahead of /usr/bin" are
# different facts, and only the second one means `claude` resolves to this box's
# wrapper rather than to whatever a distro package left in /usr/bin.
#
# THE DIRECTORY IS SPELLED HERE rather than taken from `ccrc-wrapper-shape`'s
# `WRAPPER_BIN_DIR`, and it is the one deliberate second spelling of it: that
# library is sourced BEST-EFFORT (see the top of this file), so a box missing it
# would get "the wrapper library did not ship" from a check whose subject is
# PATH — a diagnosis about the wrong thing, on exactly the box that can resolve
# nothing. The one-spelling rule keeps its mechanism instead of its promise:
# `ccrc-doctor.test.ts`'s "names the same directory the wrapper library does"
# compares the two literals and goes red on a drift.
_check_path() {
  local want="$HOME/.local/bin" p rest="${PATH:-}:" i=0
  while [ -n "$rest" ]; do
    p="${rest%%:*}"; rest="${rest#*:}"; i=$((i + 1))
    if [ "$p" = "$want" ]; then
      _dr_pass path "\$HOME/.local/bin is in \$PATH (position $i)"
      return 0
    fi
  done
  _dr_warn path "\$HOME/.local/bin is not in \$PATH — ccrc, ccd and every remedy this doctor prints resolve there" \
    "add 'export PATH=\"\$HOME/.local/bin:\$PATH\"' to ~/.profile (or ~/.bashrc), then log in again"
  return 2
}

# ── the units this box is supposed to be running ──────────────────────────
# A unit FILE with no process behind it is the failure spec §5 calls "services
# active", and no other check here can see it: the binaries are installed, the
# config is coherent, the roster agrees with the wrappers — and nothing answers
# on :7788.
#
# ── WHAT IS MEASURED IS WHAT WAS INSTALLED ───────────────────────────────
# Which units a box has IS its role (`_box_role`'s inference, D-73: nothing on a
# box records its role, and the unit files are the only evidence there is), so
# this asks about the units whose FILE is present and skips a box that has none
# rather than demanding units a dev checkout was never meant to have. A server
# box has `ccrc.service`, a fleet host has `ccrc-agent.service` and the
# cap-scopes timer, and `deploy.sh` (:416, :463) is what puts each there.
#
# ── TWO CLASSES, BECAUSE THEY ARE TWO DIFFERENT MORNINGS ─────────────────
# A stopped `ccrc.service`/`ccrc-agent.service` is a FAIL: the box is configured
# to run it and it is not running, so the PWA answers nothing or the fleet link
# is down. A stopped `ccd-cap-scopes.timer` is a WARN: everything still works,
# and panes spawned meanwhile run without their memory cap (deploy.sh's OOM
# guardrail, added after a real outage). One remedy each, per this file's
# two-classes rule — joining them would hand the timer the server's instruction.
#
# `ccd-cap-scopes.service` IS DELIBERATELY NOT ASKED ABOUT. It is `Type=oneshot`
# (deploy/systemd/ccd-cap-scopes.service), so between runs a perfectly healthy
# box answers `inactive` for it — a check that asked would report every fleet
# host as broken. The TIMER is the thing that is supposed to be running, and it
# is the thing that is asked.
#
# THE DIRECTORY IS FILE-SCOPE because TWO checks read it: this one, and
# `_check_config`'s D-86 topology branch, which decides what an absent
# `ccrc.env` MEANS from the same unit files. One spelling, for the reason
# `ccrc-wrapper-shape` gives about `WRAPPER_BIN_DIR`: two checks comparing
# their own copies of a path is the same drift in miniature, and here it would
# be worse than a drift — the two would disagree about what role the box is.
# ONE SPELLING PER BOX, chosen the same way every other platform arm in this
# tree is. The comment above explains why this is one variable and not two
# copies; macOS keeps user agents in ~/Library/LaunchAgents, and adding the
# arm here rather than at the four read sites keeps that property.
if [ "${CCD_OS:-linux}" = darwin ]; then
  CCRC_UNIT_DIR="$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents"
else
  CCRC_UNIT_DIR="$HOME/.config/systemd/user"
fi

# THE FILE A UNIT IS ON DISK. systemd's unit name IS its filename; launchd's
# is a label plus `.plist`, and the translation already exists in `_svc_label`
# — which this file can call, because `ccrc` sources the platform block before
# it sources this one. Spelled once here so the checks below keep reading in
# systemd's vocabulary, which is the vocabulary their messages are written in.
_dr_unit_file() {   # <unit> -> the filename to look for under $CCRC_UNIT_DIR
  if [ "${CCD_OS:-linux}" = darwin ]; then printf '%s.plist' "$(_svc_label "$1")"; else printf '%s' "$1"; fi
}
# THE REMEDY VERB. Every "restart it" line in this file used to end in
# `systemctl --user restart ccrc.service`; on macOS that is a command not
# found, which turns a remedy into a second dead end.
# ONE DEFINITION, in the platform block `ccrc` sources before this file — this
# is the same question `_svc_restart_hint` answers, and two copies of a remedy
# string is how the two come to name different commands.
_dr_restart_hint() { _svc_restart_hint "$1"; }

_check_services() {
  local dir="$CCRC_UNIT_DIR" u st rc=0
  local -a ok=() installed=()
  # Not a glob over the directory: `claude-session@*.service` lives there too
  # (every ccd session is one, CLAUDE.md) and those are long-lived by design and
  # none of doctor's business.
  local -a known=(ccrc.service ccrc-agent.service ccd-cap-scopes.timer)
  # ON DISK BY THE PLATFORM'S NAME, reported by systemd's. `_dr_unit_file` is
  # the one translation; keeping `installed` in unit vocabulary is what lets
  # every message below read the same on both platforms. ccd-cap-scopes has no
  # macOS counterpart at all (it caps cgroup scopes) and `_inst_units` does not
  # install one, so it is simply never found there — a skip, not a failure.
  for u in "${known[@]}"; do
    [ -f "$dir/$(_dr_unit_file "$u")" ] && installed+=("$u")
  done

  if [ "${#installed[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
    # THE FOURTH OUTCOME, and the reason it is not a WARN: a checkout with no
    # install is the normal state of a developer's box, and there is nothing
    # here to fix. No remedy under a skip, by this file's own contract.
    _dr_skip services "no ccrc units installed in $dir — a dev checkout, or a box \`ccrc install\` never touched"
    return 3
  fi

  if ! _have_systemctl; then
    local _mgr=systemctl; [ "${CCD_OS:-linux}" = darwin ] && _mgr=launchctl
    _dr_fail services "$_mgr is not on PATH, but this box has job files in $dir (${installed[*]}) — so nothing could be asked whether they are running" \
      "install/enable this box's service manager — every ccd session is one of its jobs too (see the 'linger' check above); or remove the job files nothing on this box can start"
    return 1
  fi
  # Defaulted exactly as `ccrc`'s `_box_units` and `ccd:93-95` default them, and
  # for the same reason: without these two, `systemctl --user` cannot find the
  # bus from a non-interactive SSH context, every unit answers empty, and this
  # check reports a healthy box's units as stopped.
  # LINUX-ONLY BY CONSTRUCTION: launchctl needs no session bus, and exporting
  # a /run/user path on macOS would invent a directory for the benefit of
  # nothing.
  if [ "${CCD_OS:-linux}" != darwin ]; then
    : "${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:=/run/user/$UID}"; export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
    : "${DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS:=unix:path=${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}/bus}"; export DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
  fi

  for u in "${installed[@]}"; do
    # systemd's own word is quoted back (`failed`, `activating`, `inactive`),
    # because "failed" and "inactive" send an operator to two different places.
    # An EMPTY answer is a unit systemd would not talk about at all, which is
    # neither — hence `${st:-not active}` rather than a default of "inactive".
    st="$(_svc_is_active "$u")"
    if [ "$st" = active ]; then
      ok+=("$u is active")
      continue
    fi
    case "$u" in
      *.timer)
        _dr_warn services "$u is installed but ${st:-not active} — panes spawned while it is stopped run without their memory cap" \
          "systemctl --user enable --now $u"
        [ "$rc" -eq 1 ] || rc=2 ;;
      *)
        _dr_fail services "$u is installed but ${st:-not active} — this box is configured to run it and it is not running" \
          "systemctl --user enable --now $u, then read what it says: systemctl --user status $u, journalctl --user -u $u -n 50"
        rc=1 ;;
    esac
  done

  # The PASS names every unit it asked about, so a box that answers "PASS
  # services" cannot be a box where the check quietly measured one unit.
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
    _dr_pass services "$(_dr_join ${ok[@]+"${ok[@]}"})"
  fi
  return "$rc"
}

# ── the box's own config file ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE ONE CHECK WHOSE FAIL IS A REPRODUCTION. `CCRC_FLEET=remote` with either
# agent value missing makes the server print one line and exit 1 at boot
# (`server/src/index.ts:75-79`), which reaches an operator as a unit that will
# not come up and a journal they have to go and read. Measured here, it is a
# sentence naming the two keys.
#
# ── COHERENCE, NOT REACHABILITY ──────────────────────────────────────────
# This check reads the FILE and says whether it is one the server can boot on.
# Whether the agent at that URL answers, and whether the two boxes agree, is the
# `fleet` check's question and it has three answers of its own for it. Two
# checks measuring one thing is how they come to disagree.
#
# ── THE FILE IS NEVER SOURCED, AND NO VALUE IS EVER PRINTED ──────────────
# `~/.ccrc/ccrc.env` holds `CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN`, `CCRC_VAPID_PRIVATE` and a
# Hetzner token beside its addresses (`ccd/ccrc:355-380` states the rule and why
# the parse it uses is a deliberate SUBSET of systemd's `EnvironmentFile=`).
# Sourcing it would export all three into this process and every child it
# spawns. So the two credential keys are measured as SET or NOT SET, `CCRC_FLEET`
# is CLASSIFIED rather than quoted, and nothing off that file reaches a terminal
# an operator pastes into a ticket.
#
# D-85: the reader and the path both come from `ccrc` — `_box_env_value` and
# `BOX_ENV_FILE`, whose own comment (ccrc:91-101) names doctor as one of the
# three readers that must not re-spell the path. A private copy of either was
# the brief's suggestion and is refused for the reason this file's header gives
# about the wrapper shape: a second copy of a parser whose SUBSET property is
# the load-bearing part is how the two come to disagree about a file neither
# tool's consumer reads that way. Absence is reported the way `_check_fleet`
# reports the same absence — as a bug in ccrc, in a verdict line.
_check_config() {
  if [ -z "${BOX_ENV_FILE:-}" ] || ! declare -F _box_env_value >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail config "ccrc's own config reader is not loaded, so this box's ccrc.env was never read" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — _check_config reads BOX_ENV_FILE through _box_env_value, both declared once in ccrc itself, and this check table was sourced by something that is not ccrc"
    return 1
  fi

  if [ ! -e "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    # ── D-86: WHAT AN ABSENT ccrc.env MEANS DEPENDS ON THE BOX ────────────
    # THE FLEET HOST HAS NO ccrc.env AND IS NOT MISSING ONE. It carries
    # `~/.ccrc/agent.env` (which configures the agent) and has no reason to know
    # where the server is — `_check_fleet` states exactly this, and SKIPS for it.
    # `ccrc` ships to that box too (deploy.sh:401), so until this branch existed
    # every doctor run there printed "the server's defaults apply" about a server
    # that does not run there, and told the operator to run `ccrc install` — the
    # single-box SERVER-role installer. Wrong sentence and wrong instruction, on
    # the one box in the topology that has neither.
    #
    # The evidence is the UNIT FILES, which is the only thing on a box that
    # records its role at all (D-73, `_box_role`'s own reasoning) and exactly
    # what `_check_services` above already reads. Both halves are required:
    # "no ccrc.service" alone is also a dev checkout that has installed nothing,
    # and for that box the defaults sentence below is correct.
    if [ ! -f "$CCRC_UNIT_DIR/$(_dr_unit_file ccrc.service)" ] && [ -f "$CCRC_UNIT_DIR/$(_dr_unit_file ccrc-agent.service)" ]; then
      _dr_skip config "no $BOX_ENV_FILE, and this box's units are the fleet role's ($CCRC_UNIT_DIR has ccrc-agent.service and no ccrc.service) — the server's config file does not live here, so there is nothing to measure"
      return 3
    fi
    # NOT a FAIL: a box with no config file boots, in the mode the server's own
    # defaults pick (`server/src/config.ts`). It is worth saying out loud
    # because the defaults are a SINGLE-BOX localhost server, and an operator
    # who believes they configured a fleet host has just been told otherwise.
    _dr_warn config "no $BOX_ENV_FILE, so the server's defaults apply: local mode on 127.0.0.1:7788" \
      "run: ccrc install (it writes one), or copy deploy/ccrc.env.example to $BOX_ENV_FILE and edit it"
    return 2
  fi
  # "There is no file", "there is one and it is not a file" and "there is one
  # and I cannot read it" are three different things to do something about, and
  # without these two guards the second and third arrive as a file that declares
  # nothing — a confident PASS on a box nobody measured, with bash's own
  # diagnosis on stderr. `_box_server_addr` (ccrc:315-325) splits them the same
  # way, and for the same reason.
  if [ ! -f "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    _dr_warn config "$BOX_ENV_FILE is not a regular file, so nothing could be read out of it" \
      "look at what is really there: ls -ld $BOX_ENV_FILE"
    return 2
  fi
  if [ ! -r "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    _dr_warn config "$BOX_ENV_FILE cannot be read, so this box's mode was never measured" \
      "check its mode and owner: ls -l $BOX_ENV_FILE (the server reads it as the user the unit runs as)"
    return 2
  fi

  local mode url token
  mode="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_FLEET)"
  url="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_AGENT_URL)"
  token="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN)"

  # `server/src/config.ts:154` reads anything that is not exactly `remote` as
  # local — absent included. A check stricter than the reader it describes would
  # report a box that boots perfectly well as misconfigured, which is the
  # one-directional-failure rule `_box_env_value`'s own header states.
  if [ "$mode" != remote ]; then
    local why="CCRC_FLEET is not set"
    [ -z "$mode" ] || why="CCRC_FLEET is set to something other than \"remote\""
    _dr_pass config "$BOX_ENV_FILE present, fleet mode local ($why, which server/src/config.ts reads as local)"
    return 0
  fi

  # BOTH halves of the server's own condition (`!cfg.agentUrl || !cfg.agentToken`),
  # and empty is absent to `config.ts:155-156` as it is here.
  local -a gaps=()
  [ -n "$url" ] || gaps+=("CCRC_AGENT_URL is empty or absent")
  [ -n "$token" ] || gaps+=("CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN is empty or absent")
  if [ "${#gaps[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    _dr_fail config "$BOX_ENV_FILE declares CCRC_FLEET=remote but $(_dr_join ${gaps[@]+"${gaps[@]}"}) — the server exits 1 at boot in exactly this state, before it listens on anything" \
      "set both CCRC_AGENT_URL and CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN in ~/.ccrc/ccrc.env, or set CCRC_FLEET=local"
    return 1
  fi
  _dr_pass config "$BOX_ENV_FILE present, fleet mode remote, and both CCRC_AGENT_URL and CCRC_AGENT_TOKEN are set (whether the agent ANSWERS is the 'fleet' check below)"
}

# ── the session gate: the flag, and the passphrase it needs ───────────────
# TWO FACTS, ONE VERDICT, because neither is worth anything without the other.
# `CCRC_AUTH=on` with no `~/.ccrc/auth.scrypt` is a box that answers 401 to
# every request and cannot be logged into at all (Task 5's fail-SHUT polarity);
# a passphrase with the flag off is a console anybody on the tailnet can drive.
# An operator acts on those two differently, so they get different classes —
# but they are one CHECK, because "is this box's gate in a working state" is
# one question and answering it out of two checks is how two checks come to
# disagree.
#
# ── WHERE THE CLASSES LAND, AND WHY (operator ruling, Task 9 review) ──────
#   flag off, no file      -> PASS. The shipped default of every box; the
#                             arming instructions ride the detail as
#                             next-steps text. A warning here ends every clean
#                             install yellow and teaches operators to skim.
#   flag off, usable file  -> PASS ("nothing is gated yet").
#   flag ON, no file       -> FAIL. Armed and failing shut: nobody can log in.
#   file present, unusable -> FAIL in BOTH flag positions. The difference is
#                             tense, not class (booting now / a boot refusal
#                             waiting to happen).
#   file present, no build -> WARN. Nothing measured it; that is neither a
#                             PASS nor a known fault.
#
# ── THE FLAG IS READ FROM ccrc.env, NEVER FROM THIS SHELL ─────────────────
# The server gets its environment from `ccrc.service`'s `EnvironmentFile=`,
# i.e. from `~/.ccrc/ccrc.env` — so that file is what decides whether the gate
# is armed, and an exported `CCRC_AUTH` in the operator's own shell decides
# nothing at all. Reading `${CCRC_AUTH:-}` here would report a box that does
# not exist. Same reader and same path as `_check_config` (D-85): `ccrc`'s
# `_box_env_value` and `BOX_ENV_FILE`, never a private copy.
#
# ── THE FILE IS MEASURED BY THE SERVER'S OWN PARSER, THROUGH NODE ─────────
# `_check_wrappers` already reads a JSON file with `node -e` rather than
# re-implementing a parser in bash, and this is the same rule with more at
# stake: `readAuthSecret` enforces the format AND five parameter bounds
# (D-124/113), and a bash approximation would inevitably PASS a line the server
# refuses. That refusal is not a refused login — `buildServer` calls
# `readAuthSecret` UNCAUGHT at boot, so a garbled line is a server that does not
# start, and doctor is the one place an operator can see that coming before a
# restart does. The measurement therefore runs `deploy/gen-auth-hash.mjs
# --check`, which is the same program `ccrc passwd` writes with, importing the
# same compiled module the server boots on.
#
# NOT ONE BYTE OF THE FILE REACHES A VERDICT LINE (D-140). The helper reports a class
# and a parameter summary, never content, and this check validates even that
# summary against a strict regex before printing it — because a doctor
# transcript goes into tickets, and the plausible way to get an unparseable
# `auth.scrypt` is a misplaced copy of some OTHER secret (see the helper's own
# `--check` header for the `mail.token` case).
_check_auth() {
  # `ccrc` owns both the path and the env reader — the same guard, for the same
  # reason, that `_check_config` opens with. A table sourced by something that
  # is not `ccrc` has neither, and must say so rather than measure a default it
  # invented.
  if [ -z "${BOX_AUTH_FILE:-}" ] || [ -z "${BOX_ENV_FILE:-}" ] \
     || ! declare -F _box_env_value >/dev/null 2>&1 \
     || ! declare -F _box_auth_path >/dev/null 2>&1 \
     || ! declare -F _box_sessions_path >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail auth "ccrc's own config reader is not loaded, so neither this box's CCRC_AUTH flag nor its passphrase file was measured" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — _check_auth reads BOX_ENV_FILE through _box_env_value and resolves the secret's and the session store's paths through _box_auth_path and _box_sessions_path, all declared once in ccrc itself, and this check table was sourced by something that is not ccrc"
    return 1
  fi

  # ── THE FLEET HOST HAS NO GATE TO MEASURE (D-86's evidence, reused) ────
  # The gate is the SERVER's, and `~/.ccrc/auth.scrypt` is read by nothing on
  # the fleet host. `ccrc` ships to that box too, so without this branch every
  # doctor run there would WARN that a box with no server has no passphrase,
  # and send its operator to write a file nothing will ever read. The evidence
  # is the unit files — the only thing on a box that records its role — and
  # both halves are required, exactly as `_check_config` requires them: "no
  # ccrc.service" alone is also a dev checkout, and a dev checkout DOES run a
  # server.
  if [ ! -f "$CCRC_UNIT_DIR/$(_dr_unit_file ccrc.service)" ] && [ -f "$CCRC_UNIT_DIR/$(_dr_unit_file ccrc-agent.service)" ]; then
    _dr_skip auth "this box's units are the fleet role's ($CCRC_UNIT_DIR has ccrc-agent.service and no ccrc.service) — the PWA's session gate belongs to the server box, and nothing here reads a passphrase file"
    return 3
  fi

  # An absent, non-regular or unreadable ccrc.env leaves the flag empty, which
  # is exactly how `server/src/config.ts` reads an absent variable: OFF. Not a
  # finding of this check's — `_check_config` above owns the file's own health
  # and has three arms for it — but the flag's PROVENANCE is named in every
  # verdict below, so a reader can tell "the file says off" from "there is no
  # file" without this check having to duplicate that check's arms.
  local flag="" where="$BOX_ENV_FILE"
  if [ -f "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ] && [ -r "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    flag="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_AUTH)"
  else
    where="no readable $BOX_ENV_FILE"
  fi

  # ── WHICH FILE (D-143), and the case where following config is worse ────
  # The same resolver `cmd_passwd` writes through (`ccrc`'s `_box_auth_path`),
  # for the reason its header gives: a doctor that measured the DEFAULT path on
  # a box whose `ccrc.env` redirects the server would report `PASS auth` about a
  # file nothing reads — and would do it right after a `passwd` that wrote the
  # same wrong file. Measuring is riskless, so an absolute override is followed
  # and NAMED; a relative one is refused here exactly as it is there, because
  # the server resolves it against systemd's working directory and this tool
  # against the operator's, and no verdict about "the secret" is possible when
  # nothing can say which file that is.
  local secret apath_rc
  secret="$(_box_auth_path)"; apath_rc=$?
  local via=""
  case "$apath_rc" in
    0) ;;
    2) via=" (CCRC_AUTH_SECRET_PATH in $BOX_ENV_FILE redirects it here)" ;;
    *)
      _dr_fail auth "$BOX_ENV_FILE sets CCRC_AUTH_SECRET_PATH to a RELATIVE path ($secret), so nothing on this box can say which file the gate's secret is: the server resolves it against the working directory systemd gives ccrc.service, and every tool that reads it resolves it against its own" \
        "make it absolute in $BOX_ENV_FILE, or remove the line and let the box use $BOX_AUTH_FILE — then re-run doctor"
      return 1 ;;
  esac
  # `= on` and nothing else, because `config.ts:331` is `env.CCRC_AUTH === 'on'`
  # — a check laxer OR stricter than the reader it describes reports a box
  # nobody is running.
  local armed=0 gate="the gate is OFF (CCRC_AUTH is not \"on\" in $where)"
  if [ "$flag" = on ]; then
    armed=1
    gate="CCRC_AUTH=on in $where"
  fi

  local helper="$CCRC_HERE/../deploy/gen-auth-hash.mjs"
  if [ ! -f "$helper" ]; then
    _dr_fail auth "the passphrase helper is missing ($helper), so the passphrase file was never read" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, or an install that shipped ccd/ but not deploy/ — both lanes rsync the whole tree to ~/ccrc"
    return 1
  fi
  if ! command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    # `_check_wrappers`' rule: a box with no node gets exactly one FAIL, from
    # the `node` check, and every check that NEEDED node says which one to read.
    _dr_fail auth "node is not on PATH, so the passphrase file could not be read" \
      "install Node first — see the 'node' check above"
    return 1
  fi

  # stderr is DISCARDED (`_check_wrappers`' `2>/dev/null`), and here that is a
  # containment rather than tidiness: an unexpected node error could quote the
  # file it was reading, and this check's contract is that no byte of it is
  # ever printed.
  local detail rc
  detail="$(node "$helper" --check "$secret" 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?

  case "$rc" in
    0)
      # The helper's summary is DATA from a subprocess and lands in a line a
      # human reads on a terminal that acts on control bytes, so it is pinned
      # to a shape rather than trusted — `_box_build_fields`' rule for the
      # build stamp's fields, applied to the one string that crosses here.
      if [[ ! "$detail" =~ ^N=[0-9]+,r=[0-9]+,p=[0-9]+,gen=[0-9]+$ ]]; then
        _dr_fail auth "the passphrase helper reported a state line this check does not recognise" \
          "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — see _check_auth in $CCRC_HERE/ccrc-doctor-checks and --check in $helper"
        return 1
      fi
      if [ "$armed" -eq 1 ]; then
        _dr_pass auth "$gate, and $secret$via holds a usable passphrase ($detail) — logins are gated"
        return 0
      fi
      # A PASS, not a WARN, and deliberately: `CCRC_AUTH` off is the SHIPPED
      # DEFAULT and a legitimate state for a box to be in (`_check_rc`'s rule —
      # a doctor that WARNed here would be asserting a preference). The detail
      # says the gate is off so nobody reads this line as "we are protected".
      _dr_pass auth "$secret$via holds a usable passphrase ($detail), but $gate, so no request is gated yet"
      return 0 ;;
    3)
      if [ "$armed" -eq 1 ]; then
        _dr_fail auth "$gate and there is NO passphrase file at $secret$via — the gate is armed and failing SHUT: every route answers 401 and no login can succeed" \
          "run: ccrc passwd (no restart needed — the gate re-reads the file per request)"
        return 1
      fi
      # ── A PASS, BY OPERATOR RULING (D-139) — THE REASONING IS ABOUT WARNINGS ──
      # This shipped as a WARN (the plan's own Task 9 text) and the operator
      # overruled it: `CCRC_AUTH` off with no passphrase is the state EVERY box
      # ships in — `ccrc install` writes no passphrase, by doctrine — so a
      # mandated warning ended every clean install yellow, and a yellow that
      # every operator sees on every fresh box is a yellow they learn to skim
      # past. The warnings that matter (an ARMED gate that cannot let anyone
      # in, a file the server will not boot on) are the ones that lose by it.
      # A fresh install must exit GREEN.
      #
      # The `ccrc passwd` hint is NOT dropped — it moves into the detail as
      # NEXT-STEPS TEXT, where it costs no verdict class. This is `_check_rc`'s
      # rule reached by a different road: the state is a fact about how the box
      # is configured, and the line's job is to say so plainly enough that
      # nobody reads it as "we are protected".
      _dr_pass auth "no passphrase file at $secret$via, and $gate — the shipped default, so nothing is gated and anyone who can reach this box can drive it; to arm the gate: ccrc passwd, then set CCRC_AUTH=on together with CCRC_RP_ID and CCRC_ORIGIN in $BOX_ENV_FILE and restart ccrc.service"
      return 0 ;;
    4)
      # BOTH ARMS ARE A FAIL, and the difference between them is tense, not
      # class: with the flag on the server is refusing to boot NOW; with it off
      # the box works and the one command that arms it is a boot refusal
      # waiting to happen. Neither is a state anyone chose, and the operator
      # action is the same — which is why the remedy is one sentence and the
      # detail is two.
      #
      # THE REMEDY IS THE `mv`, NOT A BARE `ccrc passwd` (D-138), and that is the
      # writer's contract showing through: `gen-auth-hash.mjs` REFUSES to write
      # over a file it cannot read, because the generation it carries cannot be
      # read either and inventing one would revalidate sessions instead of
      # expiring them. Printing `ccrc passwd` alone here would send an operator
      # to a command that is about to refuse.
      local when="the moment CCRC_AUTH=on is set, the server will refuse to boot on it"
      [ "$armed" -eq 0 ] || when="with $gate the server REFUSES TO BOOT on it — readAuthSecret is called uncaught at startup"
      # ── AND THE SESSION FILE IS RESOLVED, NOT ASSUMED (D-148) ────────────
      # This clause used to be a hard-coded `rm -f ~/.ccrc/sessions.json`,
      # printed in the same breath as a `$secret` that WAS resolved through
      # `_box_auth_path` — D-143's own defect class, one key over. On a box
      # setting `CCRC_SESSIONS_PATH` it told the operator to delete a file
      # nothing reads, leaving the sessions the fresh generation-1 secret is
      # about to revalidate exactly where they were.
      local sess sess_rc sessvia="" rmclause
      sess="$(_box_sessions_path)"; sess_rc=$?
      rmclause="rm -f $sess"
      case "$sess_rc" in
        2) sessvia=" — CCRC_SESSIONS_PATH in $BOX_ENV_FILE redirects the session file there, so that is the one to delete" ;;
        3) # No `rm` at all rather than a wrong one: relative, the server resolves
           # it against systemd's working directory and this tool against the
           # operator's, so nothing on this box can name the file. Saying so beats
           # printing a path that deletes something else.
           rmclause="rm -f <this box's session file>"
           sessvia=" — CCRC_SESSIONS_PATH in $BOX_ENV_FILE is RELATIVE, so nothing here can say which file the session store is; make it absolute (or remove the line) and re-run doctor" ;;
      esac
      _dr_fail auth "$secret$via exists and this box cannot use it (it does not parse as a scrypt secret line, or it cannot be read at all) — $when" \
        "move it aside and set a fresh one: mv $secret $secret.broken && $rmclause && ccrc passwd (ccrc passwd refuses to overwrite a file it cannot read; the fresh file restarts at generation 1, which is why the session store goes with it)$sessvia"
      return 1 ;;
    5)
      _dr_warn auth "$secret$via exists but could not be measured: this box has no server build, and the passphrase file is read by the server's own parser" \
        "build it (npm run build in server/, or re-run bash deploy/deploy.sh) — until then nothing here can tell a usable passphrase file from one that will refuse the boot"
      return 2 ;;
    *)
      _dr_fail auth "reading $secret exited $rc — this check does not know what that means" \
        "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — see _check_auth in $CCRC_HERE/ccrc-doctor-checks"
      return 1 ;;
  esac
}

# ── what this box's sessions are SPAWNED AS ───────────────────────────────
# `~/.ccrc/remote-control` decides whether `ccd` spawns a session with
# `--remote-control` — driven over the RC socket and discoverable on claude.ai
# — or as a plain pane. It is per-box, operator-owned, and seeded once by
# whichever lane installed the box (`ccrc install` writes `off`, `deploy.sh`'s
# fleet lane writes `on`; each has its own reason, stated where it is written).
#
# THE AUTHORITY IS `ccd`'s `_rc_enabled`, not this function. There the rule is:
# first line, ALL whitespace stripped, must equal `on`; absent, unreadable,
# empty or anything else is OFF, because a garbled file must not half-enable a
# mode. `_dr_rc_state` prints what that reader would decide and why; it never
# decides anything itself, and `_check_rc` REPORTS that answer rather than
# judging it, because both values are legitimate states for a box to be in.
#
# ── A CHECK OF ITS OWN, NOT A RIDER ON `config` (fix round 1) ─────────────
# This shipped first as an append to `_check_config`'s two PASS details, and
# that made a fact about SPAWN SHAPE conditional on the health of an unrelated
# file. The readout vanished on four of that check's arms — the fleet-role SKIP
# and all three `ccrc.env` WARNs — which meant the reference FLEET HOST, the one
# box in this topology that actually runs `on`, printed nothing about RC at all:
# it has no `ccrc.env` and never will (D-86's topology branch, `_check_config`
# above). A `chmod 000 ccrc.env` did the same on a server box whose flag file
# was perfectly readable. Splitting it out is strictly SMALLER than the append —
# one table entry, one function, no coupling — and it is what puts the answer on
# the box the pointers in `ccd/ccd` and the README send operators to.
#
# THE SECOND SPELLING OF THE PATH IS DELIBERATE, and it is the trade D-92
# already records for `CCRC_UNIT_DIR` (and `_check_path`'s note about
# `WRAPPER_BIN_DIR` before it): this table is sourced under `set -u` by things
# that are NOT `ccrc` — and `ccd`, which owns the authoritative copy, never
# sources it at all — so it cannot reach either tool's variable, and a
# `${…:-…}` fallback IS the second spelling with a branch in front of it.
# `single-definition.test.ts` compares all four literals in this repository and
# goes red on a drift, which is the mechanism the promise would otherwise be.
#
# FIVE PRINTED FORMS OVER THREE SEMANTIC STATES, and the last two are why this
# is a function rather than an inline `[ -f ] && cat`:
#   - `off (default)` and a bare `off` are different facts about the box —
#     "nobody has decided" and "somebody decided no" — and an operator acts on
#     them differently: one is a file to write, the other a file to edit.
#   - `off (unparseable …)` covers the file whose first line is neither, and
#     THAT INCLUDES A NEWLINE-LESS `on`: bash's `read` returns non-zero at
#     EOF-before-delimiter, so `printf 'on' > …` reads as OFF. Printing a bare
#     `off` for it would tell an operator they had chosen the mode their edit
#     never reached. The reader stays strict (that direction is fail-safe);
#     the surprise is paid for here, at the reporting layer, which is the right
#     place for it.
#   - `off (unreadable …)` is split off from unparseable because a mode problem
#     and a bytes problem are two different mornings, and telling somebody to
#     fix bytes that were never the problem is this file's own overloaded-seam
#     defect. `_check_config` above draws the same line for `ccrc.env` (its
#     `[ ! -f ]` and `[ ! -r ]` arms) — this is a coarser echo of that
#     precedent, not a copy of it: there the two conditions get two WARN arms
#     with two different remedies, here they share one always-PASS form,
#     because nothing about the flag file is a defect to remedy.
CCRC_RC_FILE="$HOME/.ccrc/remote-control"

_dr_rc_state() {
  local first="" garbled="off (unparseable — the file must hold one line reading 'on' or 'off')"
  [ -e "$CCRC_RC_FILE" ] || { printf '%s' "off (default)"; return 0; }
  # THE TWO GUARDS COME BEFORE THE READ, not after it, and that is what keeps
  # the two answers apart. A chmod-000 file and an EMPTY file both make `read`
  # fail with nothing in the variable, so a post-hoc `[ -n "$first" ]` would
  # report an empty file — which is a bytes problem with an obvious fix — as a
  # permissions problem. Asking first means everything below this point is a
  # file whose bytes really were the answer.
  if [ ! -f "$CCRC_RC_FILE" ] || [ ! -r "$CCRC_RC_FILE" ]; then
    printf '%s' "off (unreadable — it is there and nothing could be read out of it; look at what is really there)"
    return 0
  fi
  # `2>/dev/null` BEFORE the input redirect, exactly as `_rc_enabled` writes it
  # and for the reason measured there: bash applies redirections left to right,
  # so with the suppression written last a file the guards above did not catch
  # prints a raw `Permission denied` — with this file's line number,
  # unattributed — onto doctor's stderr, which this suite asserts is empty.
  #
  # A FAILED READ HERE IS EOF-BEFORE-DELIMITER, i.e. an empty file or a last
  # line with no newline. Both are unparseable in the same sense: no LINE was
  # there to read. `printf 'on' > …` is that case and is the whole reason this
  # form exists.
  IFS= read -r first 2>/dev/null < "$CCRC_RC_FILE" || { printf '%s' "$garbled"; return 0; }
  case "${first//[[:space:]]/}" in
    on)  printf '%s' "on" ;;
    off) printf '%s' "off" ;;
    *)   printf '%s' "$garbled" ;;
  esac
}

# ALWAYS PASS — this check has no WARN and no FAIL, and that is a contract
# rather than an omission. The state is a FACT about how the box is configured,
# not a defect: `on` and `off` are both correct for the boxes that chose them,
# and a doctor that WARNed on either would be asserting a preference it has no
# standing for. The two degraded forms are PASSes for the same reason and one
# more — this file's own rule is that a WARN OWES A REMEDY, and the honest
# remedy for a garbled flag file ("write `on` or `off` into it") is already the
# whole detail. Nothing here can make a healthy box exit 1.
#
# IT READS ONE FILE AND NOTHING ELSE: no `ccrc.env`, no unit files, no box role.
# That independence is the finding this check exists for, and it is what lets
# the answer appear on the fleet host.
_check_rc() {
  _dr_pass rc "$(_dr_rc_state)"
}

# ── stage 3b (spec D6): the exposure quartet ──────────────────────────────
# Four checks, one subject each, all keyed off ONE fact: does
# $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE exist? A box that never ran `ccrc expose` — a BYO-proxy
# box (parent spec :160-164), or a pre-3b box — is a VALID END STATE, not a
# fault, so all four SKIP there (THE FOURTH OUTCOME) and none of them nags a
# box that chose to stay dark. The four are separate checks and not one,
# because their remedies are four different operator actions (re-run the verb;
# run the root ceremony; read caddy's renewal log; look at the ddns timer) —
# joining any two is the overloaded-seam collapse `_check_wrappers` was split
# for.
#
# ── D-150, SATISFIED BY CONSTRUCTION ─────────────────────────────────────
# None of these probes crosses the armed session gate: `caddy` asks systemd,
# `name` asks the resolver, and `cert` reads only the TLS HANDSHAKE off the
# local listener (`openssl s_client` with SNI — no HTTP request is ever sent,
# so there is no 401 to classify into `_check_fleet`'s rc shape). Measuring
# the cert against 127.0.0.1:443 rather than the WAN path is the same
# discipline from the other side: the answer exists before DNS propagates and
# never depends on the router.
#
# ── THE TOKEN IS NEVER READ, LET ALONE PRINTED ───────────────────────────
# `exposure` fetches exactly two keys (CCRC_ORIGIN, CCRC_RP_ID) through
# `_box_env_value` and reports the ORIGIN; CCRC_DDNS_TOKEN is never fetched by
# any of the four (`_check_config`'s standing rule, and ccrc-doctor.test.ts
# plants a canary token that must not appear anywhere in a full run).
#
# ── THE CONSTANTS COME FROM `ccrc`, NEVER RESPELLED (D-85's rule) ─────────
# `CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE`, `CCRC_CADDYFILE` and `CCRC_DDNS_UNIT` are each
# declared once in `ccrc` (single-definition.test.ts pins all three), and this
# file reaches them the way `_check_config` reaches `BOX_ENV_FILE`: through a
# guard that FAILs as "a bug in ccrc" when the table was sourced by something
# that is not `ccrc` — a `${…:-…}` fallback would be the second spelling with
# a branch in front of it (D-92's trade, stated at `CCRC_RC_FILE` above).

# ── exposure: the file the verb wrote, still whole and still private ──────
_check_exposure() {
  if [ -z "${CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE:-}" ] || ! declare -F _box_env_value >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail exposure "ccrc's own exposure-file constant is not loaded, so this box's exposure config was never read" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — _check_exposure reads CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE through _box_env_value, both declared once in ccrc itself, and this check table was sourced by something that is not ccrc"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ ! -e "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" ]; then
    _dr_skip exposure "not configured — no $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE (a BYO proxy, or a pre-3b box; both are valid end states, and 'ccrc expose' is what writes one)"
    return 3
  fi
  # `_check_config`'s split, coarser: not-a-file and unreadable are one FAIL
  # here (not two WARNs) because for THIS file either state means the server
  # unit's second EnvironmentFile line is feeding it nothing usable.
  if [ ! -f "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" ] || [ ! -r "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" ]; then
    _dr_fail exposure "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE is there and cannot be read (not a regular file, or unreadable), so this box's origin was never measured" \
      "look at what is really there (ls -ld $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE), then re-run: ccrc expose duckdns (or: ccrc expose byo) — it rewrites the file 0600"
    return 1
  fi
  local -a gaps=()
  local mode="" modesaid=" (mode not measured — no stat on PATH)"
  # `stat` guarded exactly like `_check_wrappers`' size gate: never assumed.
  # With it absent the keys are still measured — a box missing coreutils must
  # not lose the half of this check that needs only bash.
  #
  # NO `-L` HERE, DELIBERATELY — and this is the opposite call to the one the
  # size gate makes (D-160), so it is written down rather than left to look
  # like the same oversight. That gate follows the link because it is bounding
  # a READ, and the bytes it will read are the target's. This measures whether
  # a SECRET is protected. On a symlink `stat -c%a` answers the link's own mode
  # (777), which fails this check — and failing is the right answer, because a
  # world-writable name pointing at the DuckDNS token is a finding whether or
  # not the bytes behind it happen to be 0600. Adding `-L` would make a
  # symlinked secret pass silently. A false FAIL here costs a sentence; a false
  # PASS costs the token.
  if command -v stat >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    mode="$(_plat_mode -- "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" 2>/dev/null)" || mode=""
    if [ "$mode" = 600 ]; then
      modesaid=", mode 0600"
    else
      gaps+=("its mode is ${mode:-unreadable}, not 0600 — the DuckDNS token must never be world-readable")
    fi
  fi
  local origin rpid expmode
  origin="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_ORIGIN)"
  rpid="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_RP_ID)"
  expmode="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_EXPOSE_MODE)"
  [ -n "$origin" ] || gaps+=("CCRC_ORIGIN is empty or absent")
  # No rp id is OWED on mode ip (stage 5, S10): an rpId must be a domain, so
  # `ccrc expose ip` deliberately writes none and the gate runs passphrase-
  # only — failing that box on the key its own verb refuses to write would
  # make every ip exposure permanently red.
  if [ "$expmode" != ip ]; then
    [ -n "$rpid" ] || gaps+=("CCRC_RP_ID is empty or absent")
  fi
  # ── D-167: exposed means BEHIND the proxy, and that is a bind, not a wish ─
  # Caddy proxies to 127.0.0.1:<port>. A server bound to one non-loopback
  # address answers caddy with nothing, and every public request becomes a
  # 502 while the unit sits there `active` and every other check passes.
  # Measured live 2026-08-22: a deploy shipped a ccrc.env carrying a
  # workstation's stale tailnet IP and took the public name down for 3m17s;
  # what made it expensive was that NOTHING said so. Empty is the config.ts
  # default (127.0.0.1) and the wildcards include loopback, so only a genuine
  # single-address bind elsewhere is a gap.
  #
  # Read the way SYSTEMD reads it, or the verdict is about a value nothing
  # uses: the unit carries TWO EnvironmentFile lines — ccrc.env then this
  # file — and for a key in both, the LATER file wins. Measuring only
  # ccrc.env would FAIL a box whose exposure.env corrects it, which is
  # precisely the shape an operator reaches for after being bitten once.
  local bindhost="" bindsrc=""
  if [ -n "${BOX_ENV_FILE:-}" ] && [ -f "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ] && [ -r "$BOX_ENV_FILE" ]; then
    bindhost="$(_box_env_value "$BOX_ENV_FILE" CCRC_HOST)"
    [ -z "$bindhost" ] || bindsrc="$BOX_ENV_FILE"
  fi
  local expbind
  expbind="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_HOST)"
  if [ -n "$expbind" ]; then
    bindhost="$expbind"
    bindsrc="$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE"
  fi
  #
  # Judged only when the value is a literal ADDRESS. A hostname would have to
  # be resolved to be judged, and a check that resolved names to decide a
  # verdict would answer differently depending on the box's DNS — so a
  # hostname gets no verdict here rather than a guessed one. Nothing is lost:
  # the shape this exists to catch is an IP pasted from another box.
  case "$bindhost" in
    ''|127.0.0.1|localhost|::1|0.0.0.0|'::'|'[::]') ;;
    # dotted-quad, or anything carrying a ':' (an IPv6 literal, bracketed or
    # not) — the two spellings an address arrives in.
    [0-9]*.[0-9]*.[0-9]*.[0-9]*|*:*|'['*)
      gaps+=("$bindsrc binds the server to CCRC_HOST=$bindhost, which is not loopback and not a wildcard — caddy proxies to 127.0.0.1 and will answer every public request with 502") ;;
  esac
  if [ "${#gaps[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    _dr_fail exposure "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE is present but $(_dr_join ${gaps[@]+"${gaps[@]}"})" \
      "re-run: ccrc expose duckdns (or: ccrc expose byo) — it rewrites $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE 0600 with both keys; the server reads the file as written, so a gap here is a box whose auth origin is half-set. A CCRC_HOST gap is the other file: set CCRC_HOST=127.0.0.1 in $BOX_ENV_FILE (or delete the line — that is the default) and restart: $(_dr_restart_hint ccrc.service)"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$expmode" = ip ]; then
    _dr_pass exposure "configured — CCRC_ORIGIN=$origin (mode ip: no rp id — passphrase login only)$modesaid"
  else
    _dr_pass exposure "configured — CCRC_ORIGIN=$origin (rp id: $rpid)$modesaid"
  fi
}

# ── caddy: the SYSTEM unit the D2 ceremony was supposed to arm ────────────
# Its own `systemctl`, in SYSTEM scope — NOT `_check_services`, which reads
# only `$CCRC_UNIT_DIR` user units: caddy binds :80/:443 as root and is
# nobody's user unit. The FAIL is the D2 pending state made visible: exposure
# configured, ceremony not yet run — so the remedy IS the ceremony, all three
# steps, not a pointer back to a transcript that has scrolled away.
_check_caddy() {
  if [ -z "${CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE:-}" ] || [ -z "${CCRC_CADDYFILE:-}" ]; then
    _dr_fail caddy "ccrc's own exposure constants are not loaded, so whether caddy serves this box was never asked" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — _check_caddy reads CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE and CCRC_CADDYFILE, both declared once in ccrc itself, and this check table was sourced by something that is not ccrc"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ ! -e "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" ]; then
    _dr_skip caddy "no exposure configured (no $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE), so no proxy is owed on this box"
    return 3
  fi
  if ! command -v systemctl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    # A SKIP where `_check_services` FAILs on the same absence, deliberately:
    # that check has unit FILES on disk contradicted by a box that cannot run
    # them; here there is no artifact of ours — caddy is a system package on a
    # box with systemd, and a container/dev box without systemd simply has
    # nothing to measure.
    _dr_skip caddy "systemctl is not on PATH (a container, or a dev box), so the caddy system unit could not be asked — a box without systemd runs its proxy some other way"
    return 3
  fi
  local st rc
  st="$(systemctl is-active caddy 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then
    _dr_pass caddy "the caddy system unit is active — TLS terminates there and proxies to this box's loopback server"
    return 0
  fi
  # systemd's own word is quoted back (`_check_services`' rule): "failed" and
  # "inactive" send an operator to different places, and an empty answer is a
  # unit systemd would not talk about at all — the not-yet-installed state.
  _dr_fail caddy "exposure is configured but the caddy system unit is ${st:-not known to systemd} — the root ceremony has not been run (ccrc never escalates, so these steps are the operator's)" \
    "install caddy from your distro (Debian/Ubuntu: apt install caddy), then: sudo install -m 0644 $CCRC_CADDYFILE $CCRC_CADDY_SYSTEM_FILE (a COPY — a symlink into your home is unreadable by the caddy user), then: sudo systemctl enable --now caddy"
}

# ── caddyfile: the COPY at /etc/caddy, and whether it is still the one ────
# D-166. `ccrc expose` regenerates `$CCRC_CADDYFILE`; caddy reads
# /etc/caddy/Caddyfile; nothing connects them but an operator running one
# command. Two ways that goes wrong, both measured live and both silent:
#
#   1. the ceremony was followed with `ln -sf` (which this repo printed until
#      D-165). Caddy runs as its own user and cannot traverse $HOME/.ccrc, so
#      the link resolves to nothing it may read.
#   2. `ccrc expose` was re-run and the copy never was, so caddy still serves
#      the previous origin, port or bind.
#
# BOTH are measurable WITHOUT reading /etc/caddy/Caddyfile — which matters,
# because a 0640 root:caddy file is exactly what the ceremony produces and
# doctor is not root and never escalates. `stat` needs only search permission
# on /etc/caddy (0755 from the distro package), so a symlink is visible as a
# symlink and mtimes compare without either file being readable.
_check_caddyfile() {
  local sys="${CCRC_CADDY_SYSTEM_FILE:-}"
  if [ -z "${CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE:-}" ] || [ -z "${CCRC_CADDYFILE:-}" ] || [ -z "$sys" ]; then
    _dr_fail caddyfile "ccrc's own exposure constants are not loaded, so the Caddyfile caddy actually reads was never compared with the one ccrc generates" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — _check_caddyfile reads CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE and CCRC_CADDYFILE, both declared once in ccrc itself, and this check table was sourced by something that is not ccrc"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ ! -e "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" ]; then
    _dr_skip caddyfile "no exposure configured (no $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE), so no Caddyfile is owed on this box"
    return 3
  fi
  if [ ! -e "$CCRC_CADDYFILE" ]; then
    _dr_fail caddyfile "exposure is configured but $CCRC_CADDYFILE is not there, so there is nothing for the ceremony to copy — the exposure file and the Caddyfile are written by the same run, and one without the other means that run did not finish" \
      "re-run: ccrc expose duckdns (or: ccrc expose byo) — it rewrites both files, then copy it across: sudo install -m 0644 $CCRC_CADDYFILE $sys"
    return 1
  fi
  # `-L`: a DANGLING symlink fails `-e` and would otherwise read as "never
  # copied", sending an operator to re-run a copy that is not the problem.
  if [ ! -e "$sys" ] && [ ! -L "$sys" ]; then
    _dr_fail caddyfile "$CCRC_CADDYFILE is generated but $sys is not there, so caddy is not serving what ccrc configured (if caddy is up at all, it is up on some other config)" \
      "sudo install -m 0644 $CCRC_CADDYFILE $sys, then: sudo systemctl reload caddy"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ -L "$sys" ]; then
    _dr_fail caddyfile "$sys is a SYMLINK ($(readlink "$sys" 2>/dev/null || echo 'target unreadable')) — caddy runs as its own user and cannot read a file inside your home no matter what the link points at, so this box's caddy is loading something other than the config ccrc wrote (older ccrc printed this form; it never worked)" \
      "replace the link with a copy: sudo install -m 0644 $CCRC_CADDYFILE $sys, then: sudo systemctl reload caddy"
    return 1
  fi
  # Mtimes, because contents are unreadable by design. `-nt` is false when the
  # two are equal, which is what `install` leaves behind — so only a
  # regeneration that was never copied trips this.
  if [ "$CCRC_CADDYFILE" -nt "$sys" ]; then
    _dr_fail caddyfile "$CCRC_CADDYFILE is NEWER than $sys — 'ccrc expose' regenerated the Caddyfile after the last copy, so caddy is still serving the previous origin, port or bind" \
      "copy it across and reload: sudo install -m 0644 $CCRC_CADDYFILE $sys && sudo systemctl reload caddy"
    return 1
  fi
  _dr_pass caddyfile "$sys is a regular file no older than $CCRC_CADDYFILE — the copy caddy reads is current with what ccrc generated"
}

# ── cert: what the listener actually serves, measured from inside ─────────
# `openssl s_client` to 127.0.0.1:443 with the origin's host in SNI (spec D6):
# the answer exists before DNS propagates and never depends on the WAN path.
# The handshake is the WHOLE conversation — no HTTP request is sent (the
# D-150 note above). Bounded by CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT for the reason that
# knob exists: a wedged listener holding :443 without answering is precisely a
# box this check will be run on.
_check_cert() {
  if [ -z "${CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE:-}" ] || [ -z "${CCRC_CADDY_ROOT_CA:-}" ] || ! declare -F _box_env_value >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail cert "ccrc's own exposure constants are not loaded, so the served certificate was never read" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — _check_cert reads CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE (through _box_env_value) and CCRC_CADDY_ROOT_CA, all declared once in ccrc itself, and this check table was sourced by something that is not ccrc"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ ! -e "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" ]; then
    _dr_skip cert "no exposure configured (no $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE), so there is no certificate this box is supposed to be serving"
    return 3
  fi
  local origin host expmode
  origin="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_ORIGIN)"
  host="${origin#https://}"
  expmode="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_EXPOSE_MODE)"
  if [ -z "$host" ] || [ "$host" = "$origin" ]; then
    # No https:// origin means no name to put in SNI. The exposure check owns
    # that finding (it FAILs on the missing key); a second verdict here would
    # count one gap twice.
    _dr_skip cert "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE names no https:// origin to ask the listener for (the exposure check FAILs on that), so there is no SNI name to measure with"
    return 3
  fi
  if ! command -v openssl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_warn cert "openssl is not on PATH, so the served certificate could not be read" \
      "install it: sudo apt install openssl — until then nothing here can tell a fresh certificate from an expired one"
    return 2
  fi
  # Candidates: loopback first (the common all-interfaces bind), then this
  # box's own addresses (`hostname -I` — the name check's source). Caddy CAN
  # bind a single interface: live 2026-08-21, tailscaled held the tailnet
  # IP's :443, caddy took the public IP, and loopback REFUSED — a probe that
  # stopped there FAILed a box serving a perfectly good certificate. The
  # first listener that completes a handshake is the one measured.
  local -a cert_cands=(127.0.0.1)
  local cip
  if command -v hostname >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    for cip in $(hostname -I 2>/dev/null); do
      case " ${cert_cands[*]} " in *" $cip "*) ;; *) cert_cands+=("$cip") ;; esac
    done
  fi
  # SNI carries the origin's HOST — except on mode ip (stage 5, S10), where
  # the origin is a bare address and RFC 6066 says a server_name is a
  # hostname, never a literal IP: the probe goes without the extension, the
  # same shape a browser sends to https://<ip>.
  local -a sni=()
  [ "$expmode" = ip ] || sni=(-servername "$host")
  local pem="" rc=1 addr="" cert_timed_out=""
  for addr in "${cert_cands[@]}"; do
      pem="$(_plat_timeout "$CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT" openssl s_client -connect "$addr:443" ${sni[@]+"${sni[@]}"} </dev/null 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
    [ "$rc" -eq 124 ] && cert_timed_out="$addr"
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && [ -n "$pem" ] && break
  done
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || [ -z "$pem" ]; then
    if [ -n "$cert_timed_out" ]; then
      _dr_fail cert "something holds $cert_timed_out:443 but did not finish a TLS handshake within ${CCRC_DOCTOR_GH_TIMEOUT}s" \
        "whatever is on :443 is wedged — see the caddy check above, and read its log: journalctl -u caddy -n 50"
    else
      _dr_fail cert "nothing answered a TLS handshake for $host on :443 at any of this box's addresses (${cert_cands[*]})" \
        "caddy is not serving — see the caddy check above; once its unit is active, stock caddy fetches the certificate itself (and if THAT fails, the usual cause is ports 80/443 not forwarded to this box)"
    fi
    return 1
  fi
  local enddate
  enddate="$(printf '%s\n' "$pem" | openssl x509 -noout -enddate 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$enddate" in
    notAfter=?*) ;;
    *)
      _dr_fail cert "the listener at $addr:443 answered the handshake but no certificate could be read out of the answer" \
        "whatever holds :443 is probably not caddy — see the caddy check above, and look at what is listening: sudo ss -tlnp 'sport = :443'"
      return 1 ;;
  esac
  # ── mode ip (stage 5, S10): the chain is caddy's INTERNAL CA, by design ──
  # Presence is proven (a completed handshake and a parseable certificate —
  # the leaf's subject is empty on an internal cert, the address rides the
  # SANs, so presence IS the measurement), and the honest verdict is a
  # standing WARN, never PASS-green and never FAIL: the chain is not publicly
  # trusted until each device has done the trust ceremony once. The 14-day
  # floor below is skipped deliberately — the internal leaf renews every 12
  # HOURS, so the floor would cry "renewal is failing" forever on a box
  # working exactly as built.
  if [ "$expmode" = ip ]; then
    _dr_warn cert "$addr:443 serves a certificate for $host from caddy's internal CA (mode ip) — the chain is not publicly trusted, by design: a local root signs it and nothing leaves the box" \
      "the trust ceremony, once per device: on this box run 'sudo caddy trust'; for phones, install $CCRC_CADDY_ROOT_CA (iOS: Settings > General > VPN & Device Management, then General > About > Certificate Trust Settings; Android: Settings > Security > Encryption & credentials > Install a certificate > CA certificate)"
    return 2
  fi
  # GNU date turns openssl's own `notAfter=` spelling into an epoch; guarded
  # like every other external tool this file cannot do without. A cert that IS
  # being served but cannot be dated is a WARN, not a FAIL — nothing measured
  # says it is bad, and the remedy is the by-hand form of this same probe.
  local end_s now_s days
  if ! command -v date >/dev/null 2>&1 \
     || ! end_s="$(_plat_date_to_epoch "${enddate#notAfter=}")" \
     || ! now_s="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null)"; then
    _dr_warn cert "a certificate is served for $host but its expiry (\"${enddate#notAfter=}\") could not be turned into a date on this box" \
      "check it by hand: openssl s_client -connect $addr:443 -servername $host </dev/null | openssl x509 -noout -enddate"
    return 2
  fi
  days=$(( (end_s - now_s) / 86400 ))
  # 14 days, WARN below it: caddy renews around 30 days out, so a cert this
  # close means RENEWAL IS FAILING while nothing is broken yet — a morning's
  # warning, not an emergency (`_check_disk`'s two-floors reasoning; here the
  # hard floor is the handshake itself failing, which the FAIL arms above own).
  if [ "$days" -lt 14 ]; then
    _dr_warn cert "the certificate served for $host expires in $days days — under the 14-day floor (caddy renews ~30 days out, so renewal is failing)" \
      "read caddy's renewal error: journalctl -u caddy -n 50 — the usual cause is ports 80/443 no longer reaching this box"
    return 2
  fi
  _dr_pass cert "$addr:443 serves a certificate for $host, valid another $days days"
}

# ── name: the DDNS record, and where it points ────────────────────────────
# duckdns only — a BYO domain's DNS is the operator's by contract (spec D4),
# so it is a SKIP, not a measurement. The comparison side (spec D6: "to this
# box's current public IP where discoverable") is deliberately soft: WARN and
# not FAIL on a mismatch, because propagation lag is NORMAL and a red line
# every operator sees for five minutes after every update is D-139's skimmed
# yellow with a worse costume. A NAT'd box has no discoverable public address
# at all — its interfaces hold RFC1918 space — and for it resolution alone is
# the whole measurement, not a mismatch.

# `_dr_ip4_global` — the global-IPv4 classifier `_check_name` compares DNS
# records with — now lives in `ccd/ccrc` (stage 5, Task 7): `cmd_expose`'s ip
# arm measures the box's own address through the same classifier, and this
# table never runs without ccrc loaded first — sourced-by-ccrc is already its
# standing premise (`_box_env_value`, and every check's own loaded-guard).

_check_name() {
  if [ -z "${CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE:-}" ] || [ -z "${CCRC_DDNS_UNIT:-}" ] || ! declare -F _box_env_value >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail name "ccrc's own exposure constants are not loaded, so whether this box's name resolves was never asked" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — _check_name reads CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE and CCRC_DDNS_UNIT through _box_env_value, all declared once in ccrc itself, and this check table was sourced by something that is not ccrc"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ ! -e "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" ]; then
    _dr_skip name "no exposure configured (no $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE), so there is no public name to resolve"
    return 3
  fi
  # Mode ip (stage 5, S10) has NO name — that is the point of the arm. It has
  # to be said in its own words: the BYO skip below asserts a domain, and a
  # box exposed on its bare address deliberately does not have one.
  if [ "$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_EXPOSE_MODE)" = ip ]; then
    _dr_skip name "mode ip — this box is exposed on its bare address, so there is no DNS name to resolve (no third party involved is the design)"
    return 3
  fi
  local provider domain
  provider="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_DDNS_PROVIDER)"
  if [ "$provider" != duckdns ]; then
    _dr_skip name "a BYO domain — its DNS is the operator's by contract (no CCRC_DDNS_PROVIDER in $CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE), so there is no ddns record of ours to measure"
    return 3
  fi
  domain="$(_box_env_value "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE" CCRC_DDNS_DOMAIN)"
  if [ -z "$domain" ]; then
    _dr_fail name "$CCRC_EXPOSURE_FILE declares CCRC_DDNS_PROVIDER=duckdns but no CCRC_DDNS_DOMAIN, so there is no name to resolve — a state only a hand-edit reaches (the verb writes the trio together)" \
      "re-run: ccrc expose duckdns — it rewrites the file with the whole duckdns set"
    return 1
  fi
  if ! command -v getent >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_warn name "getent is not on PATH, so whether $domain resolves was never measured" \
      "install it (glibc's libc-bin ships it) — until then check from any box: getent hosts $domain"
    return 2
  fi
  local out rc
  out="$(getent hosts "$domain" 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || [ -z "$out" ]; then
    _dr_fail name "$domain does not resolve — the duckdns update has not landed (a record created moments ago, or a timer that never fired)" \
      "check the updater: systemctl --user status $CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.timer (and journalctl --user -u $CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.service -n 20); a fresh record can also just need a minute"
    return 1
  fi
  # First field of every answer line is an address the name resolves to.
  local -a resolved=()
  local line ip
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    ip="${line%%[[:space:]]*}"
    [ -n "$ip" ] && resolved+=("$ip")
  done <<< "$out"
  # This box's own addresses, where discoverable. `hostname -I` is guarded
  # like every external tool here; with it absent (or answering nothing) the
  # comparison half is simply not made, which is the NAT arm below.
  local -a boxips=() globals=()
  if command -v hostname >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    local hout=""
    hout="$(hostname -I 2>/dev/null)" || hout=""
    for ip in $hout; do
      boxips+=("$ip")
      if _dr_ip4_global "$ip"; then globals+=("$ip"); fi
    done
  fi
  local r
  for r in ${resolved[@]+"${resolved[@]}"}; do
    if _dr_wr_has "$r" ${boxips[@]+"${boxips[@]}"}; then
      _dr_pass name "$domain resolves to this box ($r)"
      return 0
    fi
  done
  if [ "${#globals[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    _dr_warn name "$domain resolves to ${resolved[0]}, but this box's own public address is ${globals[0]} — DNS propagation lag, or the last duckdns update has not landed yet" \
      "give it a few minutes and re-run; if it persists, check the updater: systemctl --user status $CCRC_DDNS_UNIT.timer"
    return 2
  fi
  _dr_pass name "$domain resolves (${resolved[0]}); this box's own public address is not discoverable from inside (NAT, or no global address on an interface), so where the record points was not compared"
}

# ── room on the filesystem $HOME is on ────────────────────────────────────
# Spec §5's "disk headroom (including the backup dir)". THREE things this
# project writes land on one filesystem and all three fail unhelpfully when it
# is full: a ccd workspace is a full clone per session, every deploy leaves
# another `~/ccrc-backups/<ts>/` (deploy.sh:33-34), and `~/.ccrc/coord.db` is a
# WAL database that `server/src/coord/db.ts:144-145` says outright has NO backup
# to fall back on — a truncated write there is not recoverable from that
# directory.
#
# TWO FLOORS BECAUSE THERE ARE TWO SITUATIONS: under 2 GiB the next spawn or
# deploy fails, which is a defect to fix now; under 10 GiB it is a morning's
# warning that it will. Above both, the PASS names the room it found, so
# "PASS disk" cannot be a check that measured nothing.
#
# PARSED WITH `read`, NOT `awk`/`cut`: nothing in this file resolves an external
# binary that is not the subject it is measuring (`_dr_pkg_candidates`'s reason,
# and `ccrc-wrapper-shape:49-57`'s), because doctor has to answer on a box whose
# PATH is the broken thing. `df` itself is the measured subject here, and its
# absence is its own verdict.
_check_disk() {
  if ! command -v df >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_warn disk "df is not on PATH, so this check cannot measure free space on \$HOME's filesystem" \
      "install it (coreutils on a GNU box, util-linux on some minimal images) — until then nothing here can tell you a full disk from an empty one"
    return 2
  fi

  # `-P` because POSIX output keeps every mount on ONE line: the default format
  # wraps a long device name onto its own line, and this reads the LAST line.
  # `-k` because both floors below are in KiB — `df -h` would answer "41G" and
  # the arithmetic would be nonsense.
  local out line last="" avail=""
  out="$(df -Pk "$HOME" 2>/dev/null)"
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [ -z "$line" ] || last="$line"
  done <<< "$out"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2034  # the three leading fields are read to be dropped
  [ -z "$last" ] || read -r _dr_df_fs _dr_df_size _dr_df_used avail _dr_df_rest <<< "$last"

  case "$avail" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*)
      # A df that could not stat the filesystem (a stale mount, a dead
      # automount) exits nonzero having printed nothing, and an empty field in
      # `$(( ))` is a bash arithmetic error rather than a number. Neither may
      # become "0 GiB free", which is a FAIL about a measurement nobody made.
      _dr_warn disk "df printed nothing this check could read where the available blocks belong, so free space was never measured" \
        "check by hand: df -Pk \$HOME — a filesystem df cannot stat answers exactly this way"
      return 2 ;;
  esac

  local floor=$((2 * 1024 * 1024)) tight=$((10 * 1024 * 1024))
  local gib=$((avail / 1024 / 1024)) mib=$((avail / 1024))
  if [ "$avail" -lt "$floor" ]; then
    # MiB below the floor, because "1 GiB" and "0 GiB" are the whole range there
    # and an operator deciding what to delete needs the real number.
    _dr_fail disk "only $mib MiB free on \$HOME's filesystem — under the 2 GiB floor" \
      "free space on \$HOME's filesystem before running sessions — under 2 GiB a ccd spawn (a full clone per workspace) and the next deploy's ~/ccrc-backups/<ts>/ copy start failing, and ~/.ccrc/coord.db has no backup to fall back on"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$avail" -lt "$tight" ]; then
    _dr_warn disk "$gib GiB free on \$HOME's filesystem — getting tight (a ccd workspace is a full clone, and every deploy leaves another ~/ccrc-backups/<ts>/)" \
      "clear room while it is still cheap: the oldest ~/ccrc-backups/<timestamp>/ directories first, then workspaces whose PRs have merged"
    return 2
  fi
  _dr_pass disk "$gib GiB free on \$HOME's filesystem"
}

# ── the roster against the wrappers on disk ───────────────────────────────
# THE CHECK THAT FOUND D-69. It is deliberately stronger than "the wrappers
# are present and executable", because presence was never the failure mode:
# the fleet host's `claude-corp` is present, executable, and sources
# `.cc-secrets/claude-corp-oauth.env` while its roster entry says
# `{"kind":"generated"}` with no secretsFile at all. Every check that only
# stats a file passes that box.
#
# ── WHAT IT COMPARES, AND WHAT IT REFUSES TO DO ──────────────────────────
# `~/.ccrc/accounts.json` (the roster — the source of truth, hand-owned) vs.
# `~/.local/bin/<id>` (the wrappers — hand-made on each box; nothing in this
# repository generates one yet). It REPORTS disagreement and resolves none of
# it, which is `ccrc-adopt`'s own bias rule (that file's header, "the direction
# of every uncertain call") applied to a tool that cannot write either side.
#
# ── PATHS ONLY. THE SECRETS FILE IS NEVER OPENED ─────────────────────────
# The wrapper's secrets line names a path; this check compares that path with
# the path the roster names, and stops there. It never opens, reads, stats,
# sources or hashes the file — not even to say whether it exists — because
# doctor's output is precisely what an operator pastes into a ticket, and a
# token that is never read cannot be printed. `ccrc-doctor.test.ts` pins both
# halves: a source scan for every reading construct, and a canary token planted
# in a real secrets file that must not appear in the output.
#
# ── HOW THE TWO KINDS OF FINDING DIFFER, AND WHY THEY GET TWO LINES ──────
# The findings are sorted into two classes, and each class gets its OWN
# verdict line with its OWN remedy:
#   FAIL — the roster DESCRIBES an account and disk contradicts the
#          description. Somebody is going to act on a fact that is false.
#          Remedy: make the roster and the wrapper agree.
#   WARN — disk holds a launcher the roster describes nowhere. Ambiguous, and
#          possibly deliberate (a box may keep a launcher the fleet does not
#          drive), so it is reported and not silently passed — the same
#          treatment `ccrc-adopt` gives its own cross-check findings, which
#          print but never change its exit code. Remedy: describe it, or leave
#          it be.
# Both lines when there is something of each class, and the check returns 1.
#
# THEY USED TO BE JOINED INTO THE ONE FAIL LINE, and that was three collapses
# at one seam (Task 5 review, Important 1), measured on the reference box where
# the true reading is "one real defect (D-69) plus two deliberate launchers"
# and the joined line said "three defects": the operator was told to make the
# roster and the wrapper agree — the WRONG action for a launcher somebody keeps
# on purpose — the WARN branch's own remedy was discarded unprinted, and the
# summary counted `0 warned` while warn-class findings were on the screen.
# `ccrc-doctor.test.ts` pins it BOTH ways: one test reddens if a soft finding
# is folded back into the FAIL line, another if soft findings are dropped when
# a hard one is present.
#
# ── AN `external` ACCOUNT IS CHECKED FOR EXISTENCE, AND NOTHING ELSE ─────
# `gpt` on the reference box is a symlink to a bespoke 142-line launcher. ccrc
# RECORDS external accounts and never writes them, so lacking the generated
# shape is not a defect for one — reporting it as broken would be reporting on
# a contract nobody ever made. It is checked for exactly what ccd needs of it:
# there is an executable file at $HOME/.local/bin/<id>. Its CONTENT is never
# read, and specifically: it may be a COMPILED launcher, and it may spell its
# config dir any way it likes (`CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=… exec claude "$@"` on one
# line, or not set it at all).
#
# That is not a relaxation, it is a bug fix (Task 5 review, Important 3): an
# external id used to be required to be a script carrying a literal
# `export CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=` line, because it was fed to the set difference
# below against a candidate list built from exactly that test. Measured, a
# legitimate external account FAILED the box — `ext-bin is declared external
# but $HOME/.local/bin/ext-bin is not a script`. It passed on the reference box
# only because `gpt` happens to match.

# Membership, without a subprocess or an associative array — the caller has the
# candidate list as a plain array and needs one question answered about it.
_dr_wr_has() {
  local x="$1" y
  shift
  for y in "$@"; do [ "$y" = "$x" ] && return 0; done
  return 1
}

# IS THERE AN EXECUTABLE AT $HOME/.local/bin/<id> AT ALL — the one question
# every roster account of every kind must answer yes to, because that path is
# literally what ccd runs (ccd:7361). Three distinguishable ways to answer no,
# because an operator does something different about each: a link whose target
# was deleted is a broken install, an absent file is an install that never
# happened, and a present non-executable file is a `chmod`.
#
# D-82: the first two are ABSENT — nothing `ccrc wrappers` would refuse to
# write, because it never sees a file there at all (its `absent` decision-table
# arm writes; a dangling symlink is replaced by its `mv -f`) — and go to the
# CALLER-NAMED bucket, a nameref, so the caller decides which class they are.
# The last one is a file that exists and is WRONG, which `ccrc wrappers` would
# refuse to touch (D-82's point: a file on disk that disagrees is a different
# operator action than a file that was never written), so it stays hard-wired
# to `wr_hard` regardless of what the caller passed — the asymmetry is
# deliberate, not an oversight to "fix" into three namerefs.
#
# Returns 1 when it found something to say, so a caller can stop there rather
# than go on to ask a question about a file that is not there.
_dr_wr_present() {   # id bucketname — presence probe; absent-class findings go to the NAMED bucket
  local id="$1"
  local -n _wrp_bucket="$2"
  local p="$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id"
  if [ -L "$p" ] && [ ! -e "$p" ]; then
    _wrp_bucket+=("$id's \$HOME/.local/bin/$id is a symlink to a path that does not exist")
    return 1
  fi
  if [ ! -e "$p" ]; then
    _wrp_bucket+=("$id has no executable at \$HOME/.local/bin/$id")
    return 1
  fi
  # present-but-wrong stays DISAGREEMENT class regardless of caller: a file
  # ccrc wrappers would refuse is not fixed by running ccrc wrappers.
  #
  # `-f` BEFORE `-x`, and it is not redundant: a DIRECTORY is both `-e` and
  # `-x` (that is what the execute bit means on a directory), so without this a
  # `mkdir $HOME/.local/bin/<id>` passed the whole triad and doctor reported
  # `PASS wrappers: N accounts … match`. Found by the round-2 review, and it is
  # a narrowing THIS ROUND INTRODUCED: before external/upstream were routed
  # through here, a directory fell into the a-only arm and was caught by
  # `_wrap_is_script`'s own `-f`.
  if [ ! -f "$p" ]; then
    wr_hard+=("$id's \$HOME/.local/bin/$id is not a regular file")
    return 1
  fi
  if [ ! -x "$p" ]; then
    wr_hard+=("$id's \$HOME/.local/bin/$id is not executable")
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

# `_wrap_set_diff`'s callback. It appends to `wr_hard`/`wr_soft`, which are
# `_check_wrappers`'s locals — bash's dynamic scope makes them visible here,
# which is what lets the set difference stay generic while its two callers say
# entirely different things about the same arithmetic.
#
# The a-only branch MEASURES before it words its finding: "the roster expects a
# wrapper here and there is no CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR-setting script of that name"
# is five distinguishable states on disk (nothing, a dangling symlink, a
# directory or other non-file, a file that is not executable, a file that is
# not a wrapper — and that last one splits again into "not a script at all" and
# "a script that sets no CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR"), and an operator does something
# different about each. Collapsing any two into one message is the
# overloaded-seam mistake this codebase bans by name.
#
# EVERY ONE OF THEM HAS ITS OWN TEST, and that sentence was not true until the
# round-2 review measured it: the presence triad was pinned but the two SHAPE
# sentences below were not, so replacing this whole inner `if/else` with `:`
# left the suite 95/95 green while a real defect went unreported. They now have
# a test each, plus a third that puts both defects in ONE run and asserts the
# two sentences stay distinct — the merge into a single "is not a wrapper" is
# precisely the collapse this comment claims to forbid, and a comment is a
# request where a red suite is a mechanism.
_dr_wr_note() {
  local dir="$1" id="$2"
  # TWO `local` statements, and it must stay that way: bash expands every
  # word of a single `local a=… b="$a"` BEFORE any of the names becomes local,
  # so `p` would be built from the CALLER's `id` — which `_check_wrappers` has,
  # left over from its own loops. Measured: `local id="$2" p="pre/$id"` with an
  # outer id set prints `p=pre/<outer>`. That is a silent wrong answer (the
  # finding names the right account and describes a different file), which is
  # why the pinning test below uses an account that is not the last one in the
  # roster.
  local p="$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR/$id"
  case "$dir" in
    a-only)
      # This is the generated-account path (`wr_expect` is built from
      # `generated` ids only, above): absent and a dangling symlink are
      # exactly what `ccrc wrappers` writes — its `absent` decision-table arm
      # writes, and `mv -f` over a dangling symlink replaces it — so those
      # findings go to `wr_absent`, not `wr_hard`.
      if _dr_wr_present "$id" wr_absent; then
        if ! _wrap_is_script "$p"; then
          wr_hard+=("$id is declared ${wr_kind[$id]} but \$HOME/.local/bin/$id is not a script")
        else
          wr_hard+=("$id is declared ${wr_kind[$id]} but \$HOME/.local/bin/$id sets no CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR")
        fi
      fi
      ;;
    b-only)
      case "${wr_kind[$id]:-}" in
        upstream)
          # The upstream account is BY DEFINITION invoked with
          # CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR unset (ccrc-adopt's header) — a wrapper here
          # means every other account's exec lands in someone else's config dir.
          wr_hard+=("$id is the roster's upstream account, but \$HOME/.local/bin/$id is a script that sets CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR") ;;
        "")
          wr_soft+=("$id sets CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR in \$HOME/.local/bin but no roster account describes it") ;;
      esac
      ;;
  esac
}

_check_wrappers() {
  # THE LIBRARY IS A SEPARATE INSTALL ARTIFACT. `ccrc` finds its siblings
  # through `${BASH_SOURCE[0]}`, which bash does not resolve through a symlink,
  # so a link to `ccrc` on PATH does not rescue a missing one: all three files
  # have to land in the same directory. Said as a verdict rather than a comment
  # because an install that ships two of the three is otherwise a check that
  # silently stops checking.
  if ! declare -F _wrap_parse_shape >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail wrappers "the wrapper-shape library did not load, so the roster and \$HOME/.local/bin were never compared — expected it at $CCRC_WRAPPER_LIB" \
      "install ccrc-wrapper-shape into the same directory as ccrc and ccrc-doctor-checks (all three resolve each other through \${BASH_SOURCE[0]}, and bash does not follow that through a symlink)"
    return 1
  fi
  # D-155: the same question asked of a VALUE, not only of a function, because
  # "the library loaded" and "the library is the one this file was written
  # against" are two different facts and the second one is the one that bites.
  # A `ccrc-wrapper-shape` predating `WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES` defines
  # `_wrap_parse_shape` perfectly well, so the guard above passes — and then the
  # size gate further down dereferences an unset variable under this file's
  # `set -u` and takes the WHOLE doctor run down, every other check with it,
  # rather than failing one verdict line. A mixed install is transient but real:
  # the three files land through separate `install_atomic` calls.
  #
  # `:-` would be the cheap answer and it is the wrong one: it would put a
  # second spelling of D-81's threshold back into this file, which is the exact
  # duplication moving the constant into the library removed.
  if [ -z "${WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES:-}" ]; then
    _dr_fail wrappers "the wrapper-shape library at $CCRC_WRAPPER_LIB loaded but is older than this ccrc — it declares no WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES, so the roster and \$HOME/.local/bin were not compared" \
      "the three files ship together and must be the same build: re-run the install (or redeploy) so ccrc, ccrc-doctor-checks and ccrc-wrapper-shape all come from one tree"
    return 1
  fi

  local bin="$WRAPPER_BIN_DIR" roster="$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json"
  # D-82: `wr_absent` is its own bucket, not a third name for `wr_hard` — see
  # `_dr_wr_present`'s own header for the class it holds and why.
  #
  # A2-NEW (this task): `wr_upstream` is a FOURTH bucket, not a second name for
  # `wr_absent` — see the `upstream)` case arm below for why an absent upstream
  # binary is not the same operator instruction as an absent generated wrapper.
  local -a wr_hard=() wr_soft=() wr_absent=() wr_upstream=()

  if [ ! -e "$roster" ]; then
    _dr_fail wrappers "no account roster at \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json, so nothing on this box says what \$HOME/.local/bin is supposed to hold" \
      "bootstrap one from what is already on disk: ccrc adopt --out /tmp/accounts.json, read it, then move it to \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ ! -f "$roster" ]; then
    _dr_fail wrappers "\$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json is not a regular file, so the roster cannot be read" \
      "look at what is there — check with: ls -ld \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json"
    return 1
  fi
  if ! command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    # Read by node rather than jq for the reason `_check_node` gives at its own
    # parse: this check must not fail for a reason that belongs to another one.
    # A box with no jq gets exactly one FAIL, from the jq check.
    _dr_fail wrappers "node is not on PATH, so \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json cannot be read" \
      "install Node first — see the 'node' check above"
    return 1
  fi

  # READING the file and PARSING it are separated for the same reason
  # `_check_node` separates them: a mode/ownership problem and a corrupt file
  # are two conditions an operator acts on completely differently.
  #   exit 5 = cannot be read (prints "READERR:<code>")
  #   exit 3 = does not parse; exit 4 = no accounts array; exit 6 = it is empty.
  local tsv rc
  tsv="$(CCRC_DOCTOR_ROSTER="$roster" node -e '
    const fs = require("fs");
    let text;
    try { text = fs.readFileSync(process.env.CCRC_DOCTOR_ROSTER, "utf8"); }
    catch (e) { process.stdout.write("READERR:" + (e && e.code ? e.code : "unknown")); process.exit(5); }
    let j;
    try { j = JSON.parse(text); } catch (e) { process.exit(3); }
    if (!j || typeof j !== "object" || !Array.isArray(j.accounts)) process.exit(4);
    if (j.accounts.length === 0) process.exit(6);
    // EVERY CONTROL BYTE IS ESCAPED — all of C0 (0x00-0x1f) and DEL (0x7f) —
    // not just the three that would break this TSV (Task 5 review, Minor 5;
    // widened by the round-2 review, which pointed out that the narrow version
    // left the very failure it was closing reachable through a different
    // byte). Two separate jobs, and both are load-bearing:
    //   - TSV integrity: a tab or a newline inside a value would silently
    //     become a field or a record boundary.
    //   - THE VERDICT LINE IS READ BY A HUMAN, on a terminal that ACTS on
    //     control bytes. `"claude\bcorp"` renders as `claude-corp` if the
    //     terminal is allowed to see the backspace, so an id that is not an
    //     account can be made to look exactly like one that is. The reader
    //     used to squash the three it knew about down to a space, which
    //     INVENTED a plausible id (`acct b`) all by itself.
    // The backslash escape is the half that makes this INJECTIVE: without it,
    // an id spelled with a literal backslash-t and an id carrying a real tab
    // arrive as the same string, and an escaped rendering that can be forged
    // is no better than the raw byte. Nothing escaped this way can match
    // WRAPPER_ID_RE, so the reader below refuses such an id by name rather
    // than looking on disk for it.
    const esc = (c) => "\\x" + c.charCodeAt(0).toString(16).padStart(2, "0");
    const s = (v) => (typeof v === "string"
      ? v.replace(/\\/g, "\\\\").replace(/\t/g, "\\t").replace(/\r/g, "\\r")
         .replace(/\n/g, "\\n").replace(/[\x00-\x1f\x7f]/g, esc)
      : "");
    process.stdout.write(j.accounts.map((a) => {
      const o = (a && typeof a === "object") ? a : {};
      const e = (o.exec && typeof o.exec === "object") ? o.exec : {};
      return [s(o.id), s(o.configDirSuffix), s(e.kind), s(e.secretsFile)].join("\t");
    }).join("\n"));
  ' 2>/dev/null)"; rc=$?

  if [ "$rc" -eq 5 ]; then
    local why="${tsv#READERR:}"
    [ -n "$why" ] && [ "$why" != "$tsv" ] || why="unreadable"
    _dr_fail wrappers "\$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json exists but cannot be read ($why), so the roster cannot be compared with anything" \
      "fix its mode/ownership — check with: ls -l \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$rc" -eq 3 ]; then
    _dr_fail wrappers "\$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json does not parse as JSON, so this box's roster cannot be read" \
      "restore it from a backup, or rebuild it from disk: ccrc adopt --out /tmp/accounts.json"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$rc" -eq 4 ]; then
    _dr_fail wrappers "\$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json parses but declares no accounts array" \
      "it must be {\"version\":1,\"accounts\":[…]} — rebuild it from disk: ccrc adopt --out /tmp/accounts.json"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$rc" -eq 6 ]; then
    # A scan over an empty list passes everything, and a doctor that reports a
    # healthy box by measuring nothing is the worst failure this verb has.
    _dr_fail wrappers "\$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json declares zero accounts — refusing to report agreement nobody measured" \
      "rebuild it from disk: ccrc adopt --out /tmp/accounts.json"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    _dr_fail wrappers "reading \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json exited $rc — this check does not know what that means" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — see _check_wrappers in $CCRC_HERE/ccrc-doctor-checks"
    return 1
  fi

  # ── the roster side ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # THE FOUR FIELDS ARE SPLIT BY HAND (D-71), and `IFS=$'\t' read -r id suffix
  # kind secrets` — which is what this was — must not come back. A tab is IFS
  # *whitespace*, so bash collapses a run of them into one delimiter and strips
  # leading ones: an entry with no `id` arrived as `<TAB>.acct-a<TAB>generated`
  # and was read as id=".acct-a", suffix="generated", which is how a roster
  # entry with no id at all got reported as an account named after its own
  # config dir. Measured on both of the entries that expose it (no id, no
  # configDirSuffix); each has its own test below. Parameter expansion has no
  # such rule — an empty field stays an empty field, and its position holds.
  local -A wr_kind=() wr_suffix=() wr_secrets=()
  local -a wr_ids=()
  local id suffix kind secrets _wr_rest _wr_line _wr_i
  local -a _wr_f=()
  local n_up=0 n_gen=0 n_ext=0 upstream_id=""
  while IFS= read -r _wr_line; do
    _wr_f=(); _wr_rest="$_wr_line"
    for _wr_i in 1 2 3; do
      _wr_f+=("${_wr_rest%%$'\t'*}")
      if [[ "$_wr_rest" == *$'\t'* ]]; then _wr_rest="${_wr_rest#*$'\t'}"; else _wr_rest=""; fi
    done
    _wr_f+=("$_wr_rest")
    id="${_wr_f[0]}" suffix="${_wr_f[1]}" kind="${_wr_f[2]}" secrets="${_wr_f[3]}"
    if [ -z "$id" ]; then
      wr_hard+=("the roster holds an account with no id")
      continue
    fi
    # An id is not decoration: it is the FILENAME ccd executes
    # ($HOME/.local/bin/<id>, ccd:7361), so the roster's ids are held to the
    # same rule as the disk's, `WRAPPER_ID_RE` — the one `shared/roster.ts`
    # states. Nothing else in this check can be trusted about an entry whose id
    # is not an id, so it is reported and skipped rather than looked up on disk
    # under a name no file could have. (Task 5 review, Minor 5: the reader used
    # to make an illegal id LOOK legal — `"acct<TAB>b"` arrived as `acct b` and
    # was reported as "acct b has no executable at …", a plausible sentence
    # about an account that does not exist.)
    if [[ ! "$id" =~ $WRAPPER_ID_RE ]]; then
      wr_hard+=("the roster holds an account whose id \"$id\" is not a legal account id (${WRAPPER_ID_RE}), so no file could be named after it")
      continue
    fi
    if [[ -v wr_kind[$id] ]]; then
      wr_hard+=("$id is declared twice in the roster")
      continue
    fi
    wr_ids+=("$id"); wr_kind["$id"]="$kind"; wr_suffix["$id"]="$suffix"; wr_secrets["$id"]="$secrets"
    case "$kind" in
      upstream)  n_up=$((n_up + 1)); [ -n "$upstream_id" ] || upstream_id="$id" ;;
      generated) n_gen=$((n_gen + 1)) ;;
      external)  n_ext=$((n_ext + 1)) ;;
    esac
  done <<< "$tsv"

  if [ ! -d "$bin" ]; then
    _dr_fail wrappers "no \$HOME/.local/bin at all, so the roster's ${#wr_ids[@]} accounts have nowhere to live" \
      "run: ccrc wrappers — it creates \$HOME/.local/bin and writes every generated account's wrapper (an upstream or external account still needs installing by hand)"
    return 1
  fi

  # ── the disk side ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # `ccrc-adopt`'s own candidate rule, run through the same functions: an
  # id-shaped executable that is a script and sets CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. Anything
  # looser would report every tool in ~/.local/bin as an undeclared account;
  # anything narrower would miss the account somebody added and never wrote
  # down. The upstream account is deliberately NOT in this set — it is a binary,
  # not a wrapper, and it is checked on its own below.
  local -a wr_cands=()
  local f name had_nullglob=0
  shopt -q nullglob && had_nullglob=1
  shopt -s nullglob
  for f in "$bin"/*; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    [ -x "$f" ] || continue
    name="${f##*/}"
    [[ "$name" =~ $WRAPPER_ID_RE ]] || continue
    _wrap_is_script "$f" || continue
    _wrap_declares_config_dir "$f" || continue
    wr_cands+=("$name")
  done
  [ "$had_nullglob" -eq 1 ] || shopt -u nullglob

  # Drop a candidate that IS a declared account's wrapper under a second name.
  # The measured case is `gpt -> ccgpt`, both in ~/.local/bin, one file: adopt
  # drops the alias too (its pass 0.5) or it would propose two accounts fighting
  # over one CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR. `-ef` is a bash builtin comparing device+inode
  # THROUGH the symlink, so this needs no `readlink` — and it catches a hard
  # link, which `readlink` would not.
  local -a wr_seen=()
  local other alias_of
  for name in ${wr_cands[@]+"${wr_cands[@]}"}; do
    if [[ -v wr_kind[$name] ]]; then wr_seen+=("$name"); continue; fi
    alias_of=""
    for other in ${wr_ids[@]+"${wr_ids[@]}"}; do
      [ -e "$bin/$other" ] || continue
      if [ "$bin/$name" -ef "$bin/$other" ]; then alias_of="$other"; break; fi
    done
    [ -n "$alias_of" ] || wr_seen+=("$name")
  done

  # ── both directions, reported ──────────────────────────────────────────
  # The roster ids that MUST be a CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR-setting script on disk:
  # `generated`, and ONLY generated — it is ccrc's own shape, the one thing
  # here that is written against a contract this repository states.
  #
  # `upstream` is excluded because a 300 MB ELF is its normal shape, and
  # `external` because its shape is nobody's business: it may be compiled, and
  # it may spell its config dir in a way no `export` line matches (Task 5
  # review, Important 3 — as written, `wr_seen` membership demanded both
  # `_wrap_is_script` AND a literal `export CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR=` line, so a
  # perfectly good external launcher landed in the a-only side of this
  # difference and FAILED the box). Both kinds are still checked for the one
  # thing ccd needs — an executable of that name — in the loop below.
  local -a wr_expect=()
  for id in ${wr_ids[@]+"${wr_ids[@]}"}; do
    case "${wr_kind[$id]}" in generated) wr_expect+=("$id") ;; esac
  done
  _wrap_set_diff _dr_wr_note wr_expect wr_seen

  # ── shape, for the accounts ccrc claims to own ─────────────────────────
  local ok target wsuffix wsecrets
  for id in ${wr_ids[@]+"${wr_ids[@]}"}; do
    case "${wr_kind[$id]}" in
      upstream)
        # RECORDED, NEVER READ, same as `external` below: the only question
        # asked is whether there is an executable at the path ccd will run.
        # The upstream account's normal shape is a binary (a ~300 MB ELF), so
        # nothing more than existence would be inventing a contract nobody
        # made.
        #
        # `wr_upstream`, NOT `wr_hard` and NOT `wr_absent` (A2-NEW): an absent
        # upstream binary is a fresh box that has never had Claude Code
        # installed on it at all — `ccrc wrappers` never writes this account
        # either way (D-71: GENERATED wrappers only), so it is absent-class
        # same as `wr_absent`'s reasoning, but the ROSTER-SYNC remedy
        # ("make \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json and the wrapper agree") is
        # actively wrong for it — there is no wrapper to write or roster
        # entry to fix, only a binary to install, and `ccrc wrappers` would
        # not create it even if run. A fresh VM's `ccrc doctor` used to hand
        # the operator that sentence and the operator had nothing to do with
        # it: the roster already says "upstream", correctly. It is its own
        # bucket, not a fourth caller of `wr_absent`, so its FAIL line can
        # carry a remedy that names the one thing actually missing —
        # `install Claude Code`, below in the verdict assembly — rather than
        # inheriting `wr_absent`'s "run: ccrc wrappers", which does not
        # install anything either.
        #
        # Present-but-wrong (not a regular file, not executable) still lands
        # in `wr_hard` regardless — `_dr_wr_present` hard-wires that arm to
        # `wr_hard` no matter which bucket the caller names, so that class
        # needed no change here.
        _dr_wr_present "$id" wr_upstream || true
        ;;
      external)
        # Somebody else's launcher, possibly compiled, spelling its config dir
        # any way it likes — its content is not this tool's business, so the
        # only question asked is the same existence probe `upstream` gets.
        # Unlike `upstream`, there is nothing to tell an operator to INSTALL:
        # ccrc did not write this account and does not know what a bespoke
        # external launcher even is, so `wr_hard`'s roster-sync remedy stays
        # correct here — "the roster is the source of truth" is the honest
        # instruction for an account this tool never generates and never
        # installs on anyone's behalf, absent or present-but-wrong alike.
        _dr_wr_present "$id" wr_hard || true
        ;;
      generated)
        # Absent from disk, or not a wrapper at all: already reported, in the
        # words the measurement earned, by the set difference above.
        _dr_wr_has "$id" ${wr_seen[@]+"${wr_seen[@]}"} || continue
        # D-71 SAYS THIS IDIOM IS BANNED FOR RECORDS WITH POSSIBLY-EMPTY
        # FIELDS, and this one is the exception — for a reason that lives in
        # ANOTHER FILE, which is why it is written down here:
        # `_wrap_parse_shape` (ccd/ccrc-wrapper-shape) only prints `ok` after
        # matching `target` against WRAPPER_ID_RE and `suffix` against
        # WRAPPER_SUFFIX_SAFE_RE, both of which reject the empty string, and
        # `ok` is a literal. So the ONLY field that can be empty is `secrets`,
        # the LAST one — and a trailing empty field is the one case
        # `IFS=$'\t' read` handles correctly. Its `no` answer is four empty
        # fields, but the `[ "$ok" != ok ]` line below is the only thing read
        # in that case.
        # RE-CHECK THIS IF EITHER REGEX IS RELAXED TO ACCEPT EMPTY, OR IF A
        # FIFTH FIELD IS ADDED AHEAD OF `secrets`: the failure would be a
        # silently WRONG D-69 verdict (a secrets path read out of the suffix
        # column), not a parse error. Split by hand then, as the roster reader
        # above does.
        #
        # D-81, THE DOCTOR SIDE: `_wrap_parse_shape` reads its candidate WHOLE
        # (`mapfile -t lines < "$f"`, `ccd/ccrc-wrapper-shape`) — unlike
        # `_wrap_is_script`'s own two-byte gate, it has no bound at all.
        # `ccrc wrappers` never hands it a genuinely oversize file post-D-81
        # (`deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs` classifies one `oversize` before bash ever
        # sees the disk path), but THIS check is a pure-bash roster-vs-disk
        # comparison with no node-side gate ahead of it, so it needs its own.
        # `stat` is guarded exactly like this file's existing `df`/`git`/`gh`
        # calls (`command -v`, never assumed) — on a box with no `stat` this
        # falls back to the old unbounded read rather than going blind, the
        # same trade this file already makes for every other external tool it
        # cannot do without. Skip-and-count, not a crash: a wrapper too big to
        # check is a disagreement (`wr_hard`), exactly like one that fails the
        # shape test outright, and the mirrored threshold is D-81's own
        # `OVERSIZE_BYTES` (`deploy/gen-wrappers.mjs`) — reached here through
        # `WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES` (D-155) rather than as a bare literal, so the
        # bash side of D-81's threshold is written once instead of once per
        # caller.
        #
        # D-87: NOT `read -N` (the pure-bash reach `_wrap_is_script` uses for
        # its own two-byte gate) — measured NOT bounded here. `read -N n`
        # counts only NON-NUL characters toward `n`, so a NUL-heavy candidate
        # (a sparse file; zero-padding in a real binary) makes it consume the
        # WHOLE file hunting for `n` non-null bytes, reintroducing exactly the
        # unboundedness this gate exists to close.
        # `-L` (D-160): FOLLOW the link. Without it `stat -c%s` answers the
        # length of the target-path STRING for a symlink — measured live on the
        # fleet box, `stat -c%s gpt` = 33 while the file behind it was 7.8 KB —
        # so a wrapper symlinked to a huge file passed this gate and was then
        # `mapfile`d whole below, reintroducing exactly the unbounded read this
        # gate exists to close, through the one shape it could not see.
        # `ccd/ccrc-adopt`'s scans have always spelled it `stat -L -c%s`; this
        # one did not, and nothing said so until a test asked.
        if command -v stat >/dev/null 2>&1; then
          local wr_sz=""
          wr_sz="$(_plat_size -L -- "$bin/$id" 2>/dev/null)" || wr_sz=""
          if [[ "$wr_sz" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && [ "$wr_sz" -gt "$WRAPPER_OVERSIZE_BYTES" ]; then
            wr_hard+=("$id's \$HOME/.local/bin/$id is over 1 MiB — too big to be the generated shape, and never read")
            continue
          fi
        fi
        IFS=$'\t' read -r ok target wsuffix wsecrets < <(_wrap_parse_shape "$bin/$id")
        if [ "$ok" != ok ]; then
          wr_hard+=("$id is declared generated but \$HOME/.local/bin/$id is not the generated shape (a shebang, an export, an optional secrets guard, an exec — and nothing else)")
          continue
        fi
        if [ -z "${wr_suffix[$id]}" ]; then
          wr_hard+=("$id's roster entry declares no configDirSuffix, so its wrapper's $wsuffix answers to nothing")
        elif [ "$wsuffix" != "${wr_suffix[$id]}" ]; then
          wr_hard+=("$id's wrapper sets CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR to $wsuffix but the roster declares ${wr_suffix[$id]}")
        fi
        # THE D-69 SHAPE. Three ways to disagree, and all three are the same
        # class of defect: a wrapper that loads (or fails to load) credentials
        # nobody wrote down.
        if [ -n "$wsecrets" ] && [ -z "${wr_secrets[$id]}" ]; then
          wr_hard+=("$id sources $wsecrets but the roster declares none")
        elif [ -z "$wsecrets" ] && [ -n "${wr_secrets[$id]}" ]; then
          wr_hard+=("$id sources no secrets file but the roster declares ${wr_secrets[$id]}")
        elif [ "$wsecrets" != "${wr_secrets[$id]}" ]; then
          wr_hard+=("$id sources $wsecrets but the roster declares ${wr_secrets[$id]}")
        fi
        if [ -n "$upstream_id" ] && [ "$target" != "$upstream_id" ]; then
          wr_hard+=("$id's wrapper execs \$HOME/.local/bin/$target, not the roster's upstream account $upstream_id")
        fi
        ;;
      *)
        wr_hard+=("$id's roster entry declares no exec.kind this check understands (\"${wr_kind[$id]}\")")
        ;;
    esac
  done

  if [ "$n_gen" -gt 0 ]; then
    if [ "$n_up" -eq 0 ]; then
      wr_soft+=("the roster declares no upstream account, so no generated wrapper's exec target could be checked")
    elif [ "$n_up" -gt 1 ]; then
      wr_soft+=("the roster declares $n_up upstream accounts; every exec target was checked against the first, $upstream_id")
    fi
  fi

  # ── the verdict, one line PER CLASS OF FINDING ─────────────────────────
  # A hard finding and a soft one are two different requests to the operator,
  # each with its own remedy, so they get one line each and the check returns
  # the worse of the two (see this file's header, "ONE CHECK MAY ANSWER IN TWO
  # (OR THREE) CLASSES"). The soft findings are NOT joined onto the FAIL line:
  # that laundered "somebody keeps a launcher the fleet does not drive" into
  # "somebody broke an account", handed it the wrong remedy, and left the
  # summary counting zero warnings while warnings were on the screen.
  # D-82: `wr_absent` gets its OWN FAIL line, BEFORE `wr_hard`'s — a check may
  # answer in two classes (this file's header), and absent-vs-disagreement is
  # exactly that split: "nothing exists for `ccrc wrappers` to refuse" is not
  # the same instruction as "the roster and the wrapper disagree", and joining
  # them would either launder the absent case into a defect it is not, or hand
  # the disagreement case a remedy that fixes nothing it names.
  #
  # A2-NEW (this task): `wr_upstream` gets its OWN FAIL line too, and it goes
  # FIRST — before `wr_absent`'s, before `wr_hard`'s. Ordering is not
  # decoration here: on the box this bucket exists for (a fresh VM, roster
  # seeded, Claude Code never installed), it is very often the ONLY finding,
  # but when it is not, it is the most-actionable one — nothing else on the
  # box can be meaningfully fixed before the binary ccd execs actually exists
  # — so it leads. The FIRST sentence a fresh-VM operator reads has to be the
  # one they can act on.
  local rc=0
  if [ "${#wr_upstream[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    _dr_fail wrappers \
      "$(_dr_join ${wr_upstream[@]+"${wr_upstream[@]}"})" \
      "install Claude Code so its binary lands at \$HOME/.local/bin/$upstream_id — that path is literally what ccd execs, and nothing here installs it for you; then re-run 'ccrc doctor'. If it lives elsewhere, symlink it there"
    rc=1
  fi
  if [ "${#wr_absent[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    _dr_fail wrappers \
      "$(_dr_join ${wr_absent[@]+"${wr_absent[@]}"})" \
      "run: ccrc wrappers — nothing exists on disk for it to refuse; it creates \$HOME/.local/bin and writes every generated account's wrapper"
    rc=1
  fi
  if [ "${#wr_hard[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    _dr_fail wrappers \
      "$(_dr_join ${wr_hard[@]+"${wr_hard[@]}"})" \
      "the roster is the source of truth and nothing here edits it for you — make \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json and the wrapper agree; 'ccrc adopt --out /tmp/accounts.json' prints what a rediscovery from disk would say and writes nothing else"
    rc=1
  fi
  if [ "${#wr_soft[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    _dr_warn wrappers \
      "$(_dr_join ${wr_soft[@]+"${wr_soft[@]}"})" \
      "either describe it in \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json (adopt would call a bespoke launcher \"external\") or leave it be — ccrc never drives an account the roster does not name"
    [ "$rc" -eq 1 ] || rc=2
  fi
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    return "$rc"
  fi
  _dr_pass wrappers "${#wr_ids[@]} accounts in \$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.json match \$HOME/.local/bin ($n_up upstream, $n_gen generated, $n_ext external)"
}

# ── the running build against the stamp (stage 4, Task 9 — spec §8) ───────
# `~/.ccrc/build.json` says what is INSTALLED; the server process reports what
# it BOOTED with, on `GET /health` — the same stamp, read at startup and held
# in memory. Between an install/update and a restart the two legitimately
# differ, and until this check existed nothing on the box said so: `ccrc
# install` replaces the tree and stamps it, doctor read only the stamp, and a
# server still running last week's build passed every check while `install:
# service:` was the one transcript line that knew. FAIL's remedy is the restart
# because that IS the repair — the bits are already on disk.
#
# THE ADDRESS IS `_box_server_addr`'s ANSWER, the same one the fleet check
# asks, because it is the box's ONE statement of where its server listens
# (CCRC_ADDR, or ccrc.env's CCRC_HOST/CCRC_PORT — the exact pair
# `ccrc.service` itself reads through `EnvironmentFile=`). A box with no
# address has no server of its own to compare against — the FLEET host's
# ordinary state — and that is a SKIP, not a finding.
#
# UNREACHABLE IS A SKIP HERE, deliberately unlike the fleet check's WARN for
# the same curl failure: the fleet check's subject is a comparison a
# configured server is expected to be up for, while this check's subject is
# the RUNNING process — and "nothing is running" means there is no running
# build to measure, which is `_check_services`' finding to make (it FAILs an
# inactive ccrc.service with its own remedy). Reporting it here too would
# hand one condition two verdicts in two vocabularies.
#
# THE 401 ARM IS D-150's RULE, restated: `GET /health` is EXEMPT from the
# session gate on this build (auth/gate.ts names it — deploy's liveness
# probe), so a gated answer means an older server or something else entirely
# in front of the port — and either way the GATE answered, not the build.
# Classified from this server's own refusal envelope exactly as `_box_health`
# does; a bare 401 (a proxy demanding credentials) stays the generic
# HTTP-error WARN.
#
# NOTHING FROM THE BODY IS EVER PRINTED — `_box_health`'s standing rule. The
# sha the network sent is compared and discarded; every printed sha is the
# LOCAL stamp's, off this box's own disk.
_check_build() {
  # The readers live in `ccrc`, not here — `_check_fleet`'s guard, for
  # `_check_fleet`'s reason: this file is only ever sourced BY ccrc, and a
  # check that assumed so silently would report on a box it never measured.
  if ! declare -F _box_server_addr >/dev/null 2>&1 || ! declare -F _box_build_fields >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail build "ccrc's own readers are not loaded, so the running server was never compared to the stamp" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — _check_build calls _box_server_addr/_box_build_fields from ccrc itself, and this check table was sourced by something that is not ccrc"
    return 1
  fi

  # THIS BOX'S HALF FIRST: no stamp means nothing to compare, whatever is or
  # is not listening — so nothing is asked over the network at all.
  local s_rc=0
  _box_build_fields || s_rc=$?
  case "$s_rc" in
    0) ;;
    3)
      _dr_skip build "this box has no build stamp ($BOX_STAMP_FILE), so there is nothing to compare the running server against — a deploy or an update writes one"
      return 3 ;;
    5)
      _dr_warn build "jq is not on PATH, so this box's build stamp could not be read" \
        "install it first — see the 'jq' check above"
      return 2 ;;
    *)
      _dr_warn build "the build stamp at $BOX_STAMP_FILE cannot be read, so the running server cannot be compared to it" \
        "something wrote garbage where the stampers write facts — 'ccrc version' names the same refusal; a re-deploy or 'ccrc update' restamps the box"
      return 2 ;;
  esac
  local stamped="${BOX_BUILD[0]}"

  local addr_out addr_rc=0
  addr_out="$(_box_server_addr)" || addr_rc=$?
  local -a af=()
  [ -z "$addr_out" ] || mapfile -t af <<< "$addr_out"
  if [ "$addr_rc" -eq 1 ]; then
    _dr_skip build "this box has no server address (no CCRC_ADDR, and no CCRC_HOST/CCRC_PORT in $BOX_ENV_FILE), so it runs no server of its own whose build could disagree with the stamp"
    return 3
  fi
  if [ "$addr_rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    _dr_skip build "this box's server address cannot be used (${af[0]:-$BOX_ENV_FILE}), so no running server was asked what build it is on"
    return 3
  fi
  local addr="${af[0]}"

  if ! command -v curl >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_warn build "curl is not on PATH, so the server at $addr could not be asked what build it is running" \
      "install it: sudo apt install curl — the fleet host's own swap hook already needs it (deploy/notify.sh)"
    return 2
  fi

  local out rc=0
  out="$(curl -s --max-time "$CCRC_HEALTH_TIMEOUT" -H 'accept: application/json' \
    -w '\n%{http_code}' "http://$addr/health" 2>/dev/null)" || rc=$?
  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    _dr_skip build "no server answered /health at $addr (curl exited $rc), so there is no RUNNING build to compare the stamp against — whether one SHOULD be running is the 'services' check's finding"
    return 3
  fi
  case "$out" in
    *$'\n'*) ;;
    *)
      _dr_warn build "whatever is listening at $addr answered /health with something this check could not read" \
        "check what that address really is — a ccrc server answers /health with its build stamp as JSON"
      return 2 ;;
  esac
  local code="${out##*$'\n'}" body="${out%$'\n'*}"
  if [ "$code" != 200 ]; then
    if [ "$code" = 401 ] \
       && [[ "$body" =~ \"error\"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*\"unauthenticated\" ]] \
       && [[ "$body" =~ \"verdict\"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*\" ]]; then
      _dr_skip build "the session gate answered /health at $addr, not the build (D-150) — GET /health is exempt on this build, so whatever refused it predates the exemption or sits in front of the port; either way the running build was not measured, and 'ccrc version' still names what is installed"
      return 3
    fi
    local why
    case "$code" in
      [0-9][0-9][0-9]) why="HTTP $code" ;;
      *) why="a status this check could not read" ;;
    esac
    _dr_warn build "the server at $addr answered $why when asked for /health, so it never said what build it is running" \
      "read the server's own log: journalctl --user -u ccrc.service -n 50"
    return 2
  fi

  command -v node >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
    _dr_warn build "node is not on PATH, so the server's answer could not be parsed" \
      "install Node first — see the 'node' check above"
    return 2
  }
  # `unstamped` is a real answer (`/health` serves `build: null` on a box the
  # deploy never stamped) and it is kept apart from a malformed body: a server
  # SAYING it carries no stamp, on a box whose disk carries one, booted before
  # the stamp existed — the same staleness the sha mismatch means, so it lands
  # in the same FAIL with the same remedy.
  local running
  running="$(CCRC_HEALTH_BODY="$body" node -e '
    let j; try { j = JSON.parse(process.env.CCRC_HEALTH_BODY); } catch (e) { process.exit(3); }
    if (!j || typeof j !== "object" || Array.isArray(j) || j.ok !== true || !("build" in j)) process.exit(3);
    const b = j.build;
    if (b === null) { process.stdout.write("unstamped"); process.exit(0); }
    if (typeof b !== "object" || Array.isArray(b) || typeof b.sha !== "string" || b.sha.length === 0) process.exit(3);
    process.stdout.write(b.sha);
  ' 2>/dev/null)" || {
    _dr_warn build "whatever answered /health at $addr did not answer with the health JSON, so the running build is unknown" \
      "check what that address really is (a reverse proxy or a captive portal answers exactly this way), and that CCRC_HOST/CCRC_PORT name this box's own server"
    return 2
  }
  if [ "$running" = unstamped ]; then
    _dr_fail build "the server at $addr reports NO build stamp while this box's $BOX_STAMP_FILE names ${stamped:0:12} — the running process predates the stamp on disk" \
      "restart it onto the installed build: $(_dr_restart_hint ccrc.service)"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ "$running" = "$stamped" ]; then
    _dr_pass build "the running server at $addr reports the sha the stamp names (${stamped:0:12})"
    return 0
  fi
  _dr_fail build "the running server at $addr reports a DIFFERENT sha than $BOX_STAMP_FILE (${stamped:0:12} is installed) — the process is still on the build it booted with" \
    "restart it onto the installed build: $(_dr_restart_hint ccrc.service)"
  return 1
}

# ── the two boxes' matched set ────────────────────────────────────────────
# THE ONE CHECK THAT IS NOT ABOUT THIS BOX. Every check above measures the box
# it runs on; this one asks whether this box and the OTHER one are a matched
# set — same build, same generated roster projection — because until the fleet
# host's stamp crossed the wire (Stage 2b, tasks 2-3) a deploy that landed on
# one box and not the other was invisible. Every symptom of that skew shows up
# as a behaviour and never as a version: a `ccd` verb the server believes
# exists, a hook writing a field the server does not read, a frame field one
# side sends and the other drops.
#
# ── IT ASKS, IT DOES NOT DECIDE ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The SERVER is the only party holding both halves (its own stamp and the fleet
# host's, its own roster digest and the fleet host's), so the comparison lives
# there — `rosterAgreement`/`buildAgreement`, server/src/fleetstate.ts — and
# this check reports what it answers. Re-deriving the comparison here would be
# a second implementation of a decision that already has one, and the two would
# disagree the first time either changed.
#
# ── THREE ANSWERS PER QUESTION, KEPT APART ───────────────────────────────
# Both server-side functions are deliberately THREE-valued and both docstrings
# say why, at length: `'unknown'` means NO EVIDENCE — local mode, an agent older
# than the field, a fleet host that was never stamped — and an operator does
# something about `'skewed'`/`'divergent'` and nothing about `'unknown'`.
# Collapsing unknown into either neighbour is the overloaded-null-at-a-seam
# defect this codebase bans, so:
#     agreed              -> PASS
#     skewed / divergent  -> FAIL, one line per disagreement, own remedy each
#     unknown / not one
#       of the three      -> WARN
#     no server to ask    -> SKIP (see the header's FOURTH OUTCOME)
#     a server that could
#       not be reached    -> WARN. "the server is down" is a different problem
#                            from "the two boxes disagree", and reporting the
#                            first as the second sends an operator to redeploy
#                            a box that is fine.
#
# ── WHY TWO FAIL LINES AND NOT ONE ───────────────────────────────────────
# A skewed build and a divergent roster get a line each because their REMEDIES
# differ: one is "deploy the lagging box", the other is "redeploy both, then
# reconcile two hand-owned JSON files". Joining them hands one finding the
# other's instruction, which is precisely the collapse `_check_wrappers` was
# split for (Task 5 review, Important 1).
#
# ── D-72, AND WHY THE PASS IS WORDED THE WAY IT IS ───────────────────────
# `roster: 'agreed'` means the two boxes would run BYTE-IDENTICAL
# `~/.ccrc/accounts.sh` — the projection `ccd` actually obeys. It does NOT mean
# the two `accounts.json` files agree: `shared/generate.mjs` emits no
# `exec.kind` and no `secretsFile`, so two rosters differing only in a secrets
# file produce the same digest and report `'agreed'` (measured on this plan's
# own Task 6 fix). The PASS therefore claims exactly what was measured, and the
# thing that catches the rest is the `wrappers` check above, run on each box.
_check_fleet() {
  # THE READERS LIVE IN `ccrc`, NOT HERE, and that is not an accident of where
  # the code was typed: `ccrc status` makes the same call, and a copy of an
  # HTTP request and a JSON classifier in two files is two answers to one
  # question. This file is only ever sourced BY `ccrc` (its own header, first
  # paragraph), so they are in scope — but a check that assumed so silently
  # would report a healthy fleet on a box where the reader had gone missing.
  if ! declare -F _box_server_addr >/dev/null 2>&1 || ! declare -F _box_health >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    _dr_fail fleet "ccrc's own fleet readers are not loaded, so the two boxes were never compared" \
      "this is a bug in ccrc, not a fact about your box — _check_fleet calls _box_server_addr/_box_health from ccrc itself, and this check table was sourced by something that is not ccrc"
    return 1
  fi

  local addr_out addr_rc=0
  addr_out="$(_box_server_addr)" || addr_rc=$?
  local -a af=()
  [ -z "$addr_out" ] || mapfile -t af <<< "$addr_out"
  if [ "$addr_rc" -eq 1 ]; then
    # THE MEASURED STATE OF THE FLEET HOST: it has ~/.ccrc/agent.env (which
    # configures the agent) and no ccrc.env, so nothing on it knows where the
    # server is. Not a fault, and emphatically not an agreement.
    _dr_skip fleet "this box has no server address (no CCRC_ADDR, and no CCRC_HOST/CCRC_PORT in $BOX_ENV_FILE), so there is no server to ask whether the two boxes are a matched set"
    return 3
  fi
  if [ "$addr_rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    # Configured and unusable is its own condition — but still a check that did
    # not run, not a finding about the fleet. The SOURCE is named and the value
    # is not: it comes off a config file and lands on a terminal.
    _dr_skip fleet "this box's server address cannot be used (${af[0]:-$BOX_ENV_FILE}), so nothing was asked — set CCRC_ADDR=host:port, or declare both CCRC_HOST and CCRC_PORT"
    return 3
  fi
  local addr="${af[0]}"

  local h_rc=0
  _box_health "$addr" || h_rc=$?
  case "$h_rc" in
    4)
      _dr_warn fleet "curl is not on PATH, so the server at $addr could not be asked whether the two boxes agree" \
        "install it: sudo apt install curl — the fleet host's own swap hook already needs it (deploy/notify.sh)"
      return 2 ;;
    5)
      _dr_warn fleet "the server at $addr did not answer (curl exited $BOX_HEALTH_WHY) — a server this box cannot reach is a different problem from two boxes that disagree" \
        "check the server is up and listening there: on the server box, systemctl --user is-active ccrc.service and journalctl --user -u ccrc.service -n 50"
      return 2 ;;
    6)
      _dr_warn fleet "the server at $addr answered $BOX_HEALTH_WHY when asked for /api/fleet/health, so it never said whether the two boxes agree" \
        "read the server's own log: on the server box, journalctl --user -u ccrc.service -n 50"
      return 2 ;;
    7)
      _dr_warn fleet "whatever is listening at $addr answered /api/fleet/health with something that is not the health JSON, so it is probably not a ccrc server" \
        "check what that address really is (a reverse proxy or a captive portal answers exactly this way), and that CCRC_HOST/CCRC_PORT name this box's own server"
      return 2 ;;
    8)
      _dr_warn fleet "node is not on PATH, so the server's answer could not be parsed" \
        "install Node first — see the 'node' check above"
      return 2 ;;
    9)
      # ── A SKIP, NOT A WARN, AND NOT THE OLD "read the server's log" (D-150) ──
      # This is the one non-200 that means NOTHING IS WRONG. `/api/fleet/health`
      # is gated on purpose (it publishes roster digests, build stamps and
      # divergence), so an armed box refusing an unauthenticated diagnostic is
      # both parties behaving correctly. Under code 6 this printed
      # "answered HTTP 401 … read the server's own log", which sends an operator
      # to a journal where there is nothing to find — on exactly the box the
      # runbook tells them to run doctor on immediately after arming the gate.
      #
      # WARN is wrong for it twice over: this doctor's rule is that a WARN owes a
      # REMEDY, and the only honest remedy here would be "turn the gate off",
      # which is not advice. And a yellow line every armed box shows on every run
      # is D-139's lesson exactly — the warnings that matter lose to the one
      # everybody learns to skim.
      #
      # THE COST IS REAL AND IS STATED, not glossed: on an armed box this check
      # no longer reports build skew or roster divergence, so its silence is not
      # evidence of agreement. What makes that affordable is that doctor is
      # DEGRADED, not wedged — it prints this line beside its own `auth` check
      # saying the gate is armed, and a human reading two adjacent lines can put
      # them together. (`ccrc version` on each box still compares the shas by
      # hand, which is why the sentence names it.)
      _dr_skip fleet "the server at $addr refused this check because its session gate is ARMED and /api/fleet/health is behind it (see the 'auth' check) — nothing is wrong at either end, but the two boxes cannot be compared from outside the gate, so build skew and roster divergence are NOT being measured on this box; compare by hand with 'ccrc version' on each"
      return 3 ;;
  esac

  local mode="${BOX_HEALTH[0]}" connected="${BOX_HEALTH[1]}"
  local build="${BOX_HEALTH[2]}" roster="${BOX_HEALTH[3]}"

  if [ "$mode" = local ]; then
    _dr_skip fleet "the server at $addr runs in local mode — it drives the fleet on its own box, so there is no second box to disagree with and nothing to compare"
    return 3
  fi
  if [ "$mode" != remote ]; then
    # Absent and unrecognised collapse HERE and only here, because an operator
    # does the same thing about both: look at what is answering.
    _dr_warn fleet "the server at $addr did not say which mode it is in, so this check cannot tell whether there are two boxes to compare" \
      "check the build running there — 'curl -s http://$addr/api/fleet/health' should answer with a mode of local or remote"
    return 2
  fi

  local rc=0
  if [ "$build" = skewed ]; then
    _dr_fail fleet "the two boxes are running different builds (the server at $addr answers build: skewed — the shas differ, or one of them is dirty and is therefore not the commit it names)" \
      "run 'ccrc update' on the lagging box — fleet box first, then the server box (stage 4, spec §8): the server reads what the fleet host's hook writes and the agent caches 'ccd caps' at boot, so the other order runs a server that reads fields the fleet host does not yet write. 'ccrc version' on each box names the sha it is running"
    rc=1
  fi
  if [ "$roster" = divergent ]; then
    _dr_fail fleet "the two boxes would run different ~/.ccrc/accounts.sh (the server at $addr answers roster: divergent), so ccd on the fleet host can behave differently from what the server assumes" \
      "REDEPLOY BOTH boxes first, agent first — the projection is a function of shared/generate.mjs as well as of the roster, so an emitter change that landed on one box only lands here with both accounts.json identical — and reconcile the two hand-owned ~/.ccrc/accounts.json second, which is the order the PWA's own banner states"
    rc=1
  fi

  # The WARN class. The unknowns share one line because they share one remedy:
  # the fleet host has not told the server what it is running.
  local -a soft=()
  case "$build" in
    agreed|skewed) ;;
    unknown) soft+=("the server cannot tell whether the builds match (build: unknown) — no build stamp has reached it from the fleet host") ;;
    *)       soft+=("the server answered something this check does not recognise where a build answer belongs") ;;
  esac
  case "$roster" in
    agreed|divergent) ;;
    unknown) soft+=("the server cannot tell whether the rosters match (roster: unknown) — no roster digest has reached it from the fleet host") ;;
    *)       soft+=("the server answered something this check does not recognise where a roster answer belongs") ;;
  esac
  if [ "${#soft[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    _dr_warn fleet "$(_dr_join ${soft[@]+"${soft[@]}"})" \
      "no evidence is not disagreement — run 'ccrc update' on the fleet host (fleet box first is the standing order) so its agent reports both, and check 'ccrc version' there: an unstamped box has nothing to report"
    [ "$rc" -eq 1 ] || rc=2
  fi

  # THE LINK IS ITS OWN FINDING, and it is why `agreed` alone is not enough for a
  # PASS: the server's answers are computed from what the fleet host last
  # reported, so over a dead link they describe the last contact rather than
  # now, and a PASS would claim a measurement nobody is currently making.
  #
  # THREE ARMS, NOT TWO (round-2 review, Important). This was
  # `if [ "$connected" = false ]`, which sent `?` — the classifier's token for
  # absent-or-unrecognised — down the same path as a healthy `true`, so four
  # different bodies (no `connected` at all; `"yes"`; `null`; the STRING
  # `"false"`) each produced `PASS fleet: … in remote mode, connected` — a
  # verdict asserting the opposite of what the last one says. The justification
  # written here was wrong as well as the code: `shared/api.ts:1484` declares
  # `connected` REQUIRED, so absence is a malformed answer, and the
  # absence-permits rule belongs to the optional pair `roster?`/`build?` and
  # licenses nothing here. `mode` and `build`/`roster` already WARN on a value
  # they do not recognise; this was the one field where an unrecognised answer
  # became a claim, and `_box_health` had carried the distinction all along —
  # narrowing it here is exactly the adapter rule CLAUDE.md calls its
  # highest-yield one.
  case "$connected" in
    true) ;;
    false)
      _dr_warn fleet "the server at $addr is not currently connected to the fleet host, so its build and roster answers describe the last contact rather than now" \
        "bring the link back up: on the fleet host, systemctl --user is-active ccrc-agent.service, then re-run this check"
      [ "$rc" -eq 1 ] || rc=2 ;;
    *)
      _dr_warn fleet "the server at $addr did not say whether it is in contact with the fleet host, so its build and roster answers cannot be trusted to describe now" \
        "check what is answering at $addr — /api/fleet/health states 'connected' as a JSON boolean (shared/api.ts declares it required), so a missing or non-boolean one means a proxy, an older build, or something that is not a ccrc server"
      [ "$rc" -eq 1 ] || rc=2 ;;
  esac

  if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
    return "$rc"
  fi
  _dr_pass fleet "the two boxes agree — same build, and the same generated ~/.ccrc/accounts.sh (measured by the server at $addr, in remote mode, connected)"
}
